        <p>the Land ore American fcr the most part bat they include in their number German, Belgian, English, French and eves Greek mueiciaus -or did when last they came my way. Finally a word to the conductor's methods. They are uncon- ventional enough, it is true, hot they are equally eff.ctive. The left hand, in white glove, plays an important part, and both arms are wielded freely. Yet there is no awkwardness ; the movements are full (of rhythm. While his two /wine are leading his men on to their triumphs. Sunsa himself stand• at cool and Unperturbed an Jupiter in the clouds. Thus a colleague of mine expressed it when last the " freak band, aa it in often described, viaited our shores. A ROYAL SOLDIER.</p>
        <p>Since the last isyue of this paper the appointment ! if the Duke of Connaught to be t icieertior-uener d of Canada has been officially announced. As one London correspondent writes —All his life the fluke has been known as a serious and practical soldier, and few men have worked harder at his profession. At Tehel-Kebir, in the Soudan campaign of ISS2, he commanded the Guards, and some days after the engagement of Loid Wolseley, watching the Duke pass across the great square in Cairo, remarked to a bystander, "There goes thehardest-working officer in my army.- When first I nose him—l suppose it must be about 3,1 years ago—the Duke was on the march with his regiment as plain Major, but, of course, notwithstonding his subordinate position as to military I rank, he was the cynosure of all eyes as he passed through the town. be Chief Coimlahle was a most ! unpopular peitionsige, and as he rode near the Duke during the progress through the main streets, there were hisses. Prince Arthur not unnaturally inter- preted the untimely demonstration as a personal ! affront, and the local circumstances had to be explained '1 to him. SHOPPING WEEK.</p>
        <p>May I without impertinence second the plea for a Warrington Shopping Week ? I have bad experi- ence of organisations of the kind cis-where and they have invariably been a tended by success. The !ROA° enjey shopping weeks and tradesmen who have tried it generally contrive to make it an annual event. I have many friends who will travel miles and incur not a little expense to visit neighbouring towns on that week of the year. NOTHING NEW.</p>
        <p>I suppose that one of them days they will make out that Pompeii had its shopping week. Men are con- stantly accumulating evidence in support of the ancient', say ing that ''there is nothing new under the sun." Not long ago, at the British Schools of Com- merce. London, Mr. Oliver ll'Ewen lecturing on the " Evolution of Shorthand," traced the progress of the art from the time of Tyro, Cicero's freedman— who invented the first known te-stem of shorthand about the year 50 n c.—up to the close of the 18th century, when Byron published his improvement on all preceding systems. Mr. M'Ewan made the inter- esting suggestion that St. Luke in all probability, wrote Tyro's shorthand, or some modification of it, and that by its mean, he put on record the actual words of Jesus Christ and of the Apostles. This, he thought, might explain the circurostautial character of SE. Luke's Gospel, as well no the Acts of the Apostles, comtnonly attributed to St. Luke. Moreover, even c taxt-al.s. excepting the motive power, are not nearly so new 8 in, most people think, says one writer. The Chines, Rome half-dozen centuries ago, had • cart with an attachment that measured the long miles the panting coolits covered, and it may have been a common thing for a Roman youth to spring into a "taxi "direct 'To the baths !" and then pay at so much a mile for the ride. It is recorded that Emperor Pertinax, in order to procure money for a donation to the metorian guard, sold, at a great auction, lasting nine days, the magnificent garments, fun niture, jewels, curiosities, and works of art with which Commodes bad filled the palace. In an inventory of the things sold during the auction there is the following item :—" Carriages, which had en n- trivances to measure the distsuce over which they were driven, and to count, the hours smnt in the j. urnev. Whatever the device was, it passed out of the knowledge of men with the down- fall of Rome; for the credit of the invention of the "chariot way-wiser" is given in modern history to some member of the hoyal Society, and the date fixed at 16112. Again, several if the famous sayings which are popularly supposed to have originated with well-known contemporaries of our own were actuslly uttered in days of which our fathei• have told us and in the old times before them. Perhaps I have previously mentioned that Rudyard Kipling s " That's another story " really belongs to Sterne. I have lately read that the American papers have been crediting Archie, Mr. Roosevelt's son, with a witticism originally used at the expense of Caatelar You want to know what kind of man my father is ? " said Archie - this is the American variant of the Cuteiar legend. " Well, he is this kind of a man. If he were invited to • wedding he would want to be the bride ; if he were invited to • funeral be would want to be the corpse." A correspondent of "The 'limes" recently wrete "Ito you or any of votir leaders km."' who oriainated the saying, 'All things come in time to him who knows how to wait'? I am told it was used on remarkable occasions by both Disraeli and 'lidera, and I find it is at least as old a• September 10th, 13T1. In a recent visit to the Beanchamp Tower I discovered the saying cot in the acne wall of the cell in which, Charles bailly was imprimoed. He was detected at Dover sainggling correspondence for Mary Queen of Scots. The characters are carved with beautiful preciaion ; he remark, that it is not adversity kill. men, but the want of patience under adv., sity,' and then in old French is the plumes in question." Once more, and lastly, as the preachers any, though having in mind last week's covenent. I many mean it. Macaulay's schoolboy's knowledge and wisdom were supposed to he equal to the wisdom of Solomon. And yet Mimeo's. did not invent the omniscient schoolboy, for Robert Burton, who lived about two centuries before the Whig historian, mu lly had the hardi- hood to write to his Anatomy of Melancholy "Every schoolboy bath that famous testament of Grunnius Corecotta Po, cellos at his finger.' ends."</p>
        <p>COM NI IA L FA I tray.s.—Acoording Nfercanti le Gazette," the number of mason published in Great Britain and Ireland di weeks ending Saturday, January 2ith, • number in the corresponding four weeks was AK Ihotriog a decrease of 15, being a in 1911, to date, of 13.</p>
        <p>a m-113uk</p>
