      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-04_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>On Welnes.la evening, the Rev. .1 E. Rattenbury, who bears an honoured Methodist name, lectured at Padgate on Hugh Price Hughes—concerning whom at least one amusing story has been told in this column. I well remember the first cceasion on which I reported the famous and lamented evangelist. It was years before he entered upon his great missionary enterprise in West London. Hughes hail COMO from Oxford to a northern industrial centre to address a big vemperanze gathering. the into Mr. Edward Crossley, M.P., presided—which was a mistake from the point LI view of the promoters, for (although he was an enthusiastic astronomer— one of the best telescopes now in use at the licks Observatoty was preseented by him) he was quite notorious for becoming fulgetty by p.m. In order to retain the audience and ensure a success- ful meeting. Mr. Price Hughes was kept back till last, so that he had on!y just begun Si, warm to his subject when Mr. Crossley became uncomfortable and anxious to wind op the proceedings. At last he plucked desperately at the coat tails of the impassioned speaker, but Hughes, throwing him eff, exclaimed in his masterful way, "I will go on, Mr. Chairman,"and his bearersappreciating the situation. applauded sympathetically and encouragingly, glad that for once a local autocrat whom most public speakers in those parts had learned to fear and obey had net his match. Pressmen who had experience of him have reason to cherish the memory of Hugh Price Hughes. for he was always careful to arrange for the comfort and convenience of reporters attending meetings with. which he had to do. I know of none to compare in this respect with the late editor of the Methodist Times but that other born journalist, Mr. Stead. SNUFF-TAKING.</p>
        <p>This most objectionable habit is not what it was at the period to which I have alluded. At the very meeting mentioned above, one speaker made the trite remark that if the Almighty had intended man to make a dust bin of his nose Ile would have turned that organ th, other way up. Occupying a conspicu- ous position in the front benches was the coroner of that district. A prodigious snuffer, he was no impressed by the observation that he then and there registered a solemn vow to for ever have done with his Tom Brick or Top Mill, or whatever his favourite mixture wee. He persevered for quite • long while and then at an inquest, I heard him in little above a whisper ask. " Does any gentlemen present happen to carry • snuff box.- arid vrhen a juror promptly pro- duced one lie was visilt!y relieved. A few weeks later It was only too apparent that our coroner was as snuffy as ever—as snuffy indeed, 14 a venerable archdeacon of my acquaintance who carried the muff loose in his vest pocket! TEMPORARY INSANITY.</p>
        <p>Another Coroner whom I have known many years insists that is a meaningless terra. He declares that there is no such addition as that the faviateite temporary insanity" of the average British jury- man. He tells me that he never accepts it ; in fact, he has always refu-ed point blank to legioter it. He is also fond of reminding on that ho!ders of his ancient office have powers poste seed by no colter legal functionary excepting the Judges of the High Court, therefore, it behoves one to criticise here with discretion. May no would-he suicide really be deemed temporarily insane? What of the unfortunate fellow who at our Borough Police Court last week was charged with attempting self-4lestruction by cutting his throat, and who, immediately realising the madness of his act, shouted to his mother and so saved his life? Jun, about the time that the scene was being enacted in Warringt‘n, I happened to witness a similar one on the canal bank several miles away. A physically-powerful labouring man who was approaching from the or poeite direction suddenly (tang himself into the water, fully dressed as he was, so that it was evidently a raw, of attempted self- murder. However, he at ores began to cry lustily for help, and—though with difficulty got nut of the water and up the bank on eccount of hie bulk—he was plainly relieved and thankful to his reg.uers.preferring to take his chances with the rolice, who wanted him on Another charge. In each of thlbee instances a victim of strange delusions seems to have obeyed a momentarily ungovernable impulse—in other words, to have had a fit of temporary insanity. SOC.SA'S PROJECTED VISIT. . . .</p>
        <p>This is due next Monday at the War, ington Hippo- drome. John Philip SOY 9-4 has been de.ct ibed as the Barnum of music. His is indeed a unique band. and he understands every gradation and Ind, of expres- Mon, and every combination of which it is capable. At one of his concerts there is no escape from his personality. It pervades everything ; and it makes little difference who the composer may be—the rendering is bound to he Sounsesque. As was pointed out two or three yenta ago, when he was ins part of the country where I then was, this individual- ity is so pronounced at timer., and some of the effects produced are so ext mord ins rythe t the listener wonders whether he is not the victim of a huge practice! joke. Yet it was • wonderful, a tremendous performance that was given that night. Each one of the great array of instruments wits under the absolute control of the gentleman with the baton and the gloved left hand. The drilling has been brought to the pitch of perfection, and this is not merely in the keeping of time. The rhythm of every phrase in accurate and finished, mid the manner in which the climaxes are led up to is pert inularly striking. Yet, after all, one was led to doubt whether Sou-a takes himself seriously or expects his band to be compared to the conventional orchestra. Let me jut indicate how it was made up when heard it. There were Mt-three instruments in addition to an unusually well-stocked battery of percuesion devices. rho -imeM body of the orchestra comprised 10 B clarinets, which were reinforced by B fiat, alto, and E flat clarinets. There were the quartette of Ilium two oboes. one English horn, two bassoonu. four sax - phones, one surrusophone m kind of double bass ton in brass), four c. raids, two trumpetn, four French horns, three trombones, two etiolionionm, bur bass tubas, and a " Sousaphone." The last mimed instru- ment was one possessing a huge brass funnel and was mationed behind the conductors tonne. It has a wonderful tone, resembling the bourdou pipes of an organ. The clarinets austam the share which in a full orchestra falls to tine •iolins and violas. The members</p>
