      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-04_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>PRACTICAL POULTRY FARMING.</head>
        <p>PRACTICAL POULTRY FARMING.</p>
        <p>(By Redcap)</p>
        <p>TAINTED GRASS RUNS.</p>
        <p>This is the time of the year to give grass runs a refl. Very soon now the chicks will make their apposraeoe consequently ycit should have. sweet grass ran to rear them on. The best method of renovating an old grass run is as follows :—lieep your stock either in the scratching or in any other reserve quarters while you treat the old pen. Then take some new lime and scatter well over the surface of the grass. After a few days get an iron rake and well work the lime into the surface soil. The action of the lime will work wonders. It will not only exterminate dangerous germ. but thoroughly rejuvenate the soil, and a good crop of sweet young grabs will follow in due course as a result of the tieatment. If yon have fairly well of out-door accom- modation I should recommetid you to keep such lime- treated runs solely for the young stock. Nothing ie dangeroue, and nothing invites disease quicker than tainted imm. If this advice ia lakes and acted upon early in the mason, much trouble, and expense will be saved later on. It may be, however, that many of you have only one pen wherein to operate To each I would advise that lielf the ruu be so treated, and later in the amnion the other portion could be dealt with. When one has only a small run and where it ict seem- ingly impossible to spare only half the room it in • difficult matter. When such is the cum it is far better to cover the whole tun entirely. It ie always far easier to treat a dry ran than one which is exposed to all weathers. 1 em an ardent advocate of covered runs and a small stock. I mean small in numbers. Some of the very best results are obtained from. say, • pen 010 dOIIINI totals kept IP a good sized ecratchiug shed, especially in winter, More often than not when fowls hares free range, end have also covered runs, they are to be found in the latter moat of their time. Hays you not observed this about a farmyard ? Look how the' Hock to the barn and implement sheds. Every coustruction which is likely to temper the cold winds is welcomed by cur feathered friends. Two eesentials for succeseful egg production are a good dry earth run and a sweet gram run for ionising purposes.</p>
        <p>54,F1,111:1.1.E1.</p>
        <p>' The bark-carder especially at this SIMS= of the year is often &amp;appointed on finding so many soft-shelled , eggs. Shell-leas eggs are often guard by a poor supply of shell-forming material in the diet. Some- times the cause it through some derangement of the egg organs. Another and very general cause is over. feeding. I have had pantie il experience of the latter just recently. Personal attention to my own small stock has not been possible of Lite, consequently my Silkies have been rather "killed with kindness" by my substitute, and I have had quite a number of soft-shelled eggo. The came I know to he over- feeding. It is needless for me toadd that soft-shelled eggs are quite valor-lees for marketing purposes. Not only so, hut I also find that a young pullet only lays such with difficulty, and a certain amount of danger. I have hail to treat one this week for egg-binding, the egg being quite shell-less, It is easier for • fully developed egg to pass through the egg-pearage than it is for • ben to paw a soft egg. To evoid shell-less eggs you most not overfeed, give your stock plenty of sheildurming material in the form of oyster-shell grit, and a •upply of old mortar. Here again. we realise the value of lime. but in another form. Never give your fowls new mortar. For while new lime is invaluable for the purpose of treating the lane, it is just as daogetous to the constitution of the fowl. Lime in some form, however, must be given. To substantiate the necessity, let me remind you that ten ordinary sized eggs actually contain about two ounces, of pure lime. This must be taxon ibto the fowls' body in some form ere it can carry out the natural function of perfect egg-prodiicti,n. Fowls on a bee run can generally obtatu a sufficient supply of shell. forming material, but these in coufined areas have to be artificially supplied.</p>
        <p>It will perhaps interest you ill give a little apace to talk about the new breeds of poultry. Bounty fanciers of late 'emu to have bad • tether had dose of the" blues" We have now gr t the BM. Wyandotte, the Blue Leghorn, and a revival of the tld Blue lengshan. 'the Blue Wyandotte embodies all the chsracteriatics of the Wyandotte family, cameo of course in colour. The variety is at present only in its infancy, and with the advent of no many new breeds and varieties one wonders with curtord'y whether sr not it will ever become prpalar. Of its asefulnese I have little rkubt, tor the whole family of Wyandottrs are devidodly useful fowls. Might I remark, by the way, all nme breeds are more useful as utility birds in their earlier stages of production than at a later period. Out. crowing for tape and colour has to be adopted, and the infusion of other blood i•calculated to he of benefit for utilitarian purposes. This remark may le useful in guiding those who doubt the purchasing of surplus stock from the originators of new breed.. I wonder how often zniretnarked bitds have made the name of many a new brted as utility birds? When rokur and type are tined in—breeding doe. 'lie req. But this, mark 3ou, is When all uaeful points are sacrificed. Now, Blue W 3 andotte fanciers! As you pr.,ceed to perfect sour new lore, keep the egg listsket In mind. It paid° then I shall predict • successful future for the he' Dot."</p>
        <p>TIIF. 01.1.7 E LEGMAN,</p>
        <p>..... Like the rest of the Leghorn family the " Blue " cannot help becoming en egg-basket filler. Somehow I think this variety will serge ahead. If it had not been for euch a diversity of opinion when first fixing the shade in colour this variety would have been bett, established to-day than it is. It happened that a few breeders bad fixed on one amide and some on another, each had dole fairly well with their respective colours and likewh.e all wanted toclaint predominance as the breeder of what should be a proper ratidard. General disagreement ended in the unfortunate Blue Leghorn losing ground for a time. Now, however, it is again routing to the front and next seeaton will -produce not a small number of adherents to the Blue Leghorn. We have still another new variety of th Leghorn family in she</p>
        <p>lio4K-, MIRED lILVE 1.F.011011N. Extreme hardiness and wonderful laying quill, lessee claimed or this variety and Ido flit wonder. I am without personal knowlelge as to it. origin, hot I should surmise that the Black Wyandotte has had a lot to din the construction of our new btesd. Where else could the rose t,trib I e got consistent with leg and feather coluttr and, if lam not insulting , the rose-cosub leghorn fanciers. what c..nld be a finer cross for laying purpow•ss than the Leghorn-Wyandotte? Personsily I welcome this new breed. It will he better fit ed to stand the winter frost.. Thor., who have kept the single comb Black Leghorn mill hare experienced tome difficulty with front-buttes combs An important point for breeders to guard against is in not getting the rose comb too large I ton convinced that. mimes:al future awaits the Rose Comb Black Leghorn.</p>
        <p>THE DEEi LANGSHAN.</p>
        <p>I have already spoken of a revival of the Blue Langshas. Might I say that it is chiefly from • fanciers point . f view. Perhaps it is because the colour is likely to hosm. But even so, any new breed or va.iely of breeds has to have certain utility points to recommend it ; otherwise it is bound to fail. Now very few•people (speaking generally) have ever heard of the Blue Langahan at all. It is not au all-round breed, and I dolibt if it will ever become popular. I Mat be candid and soy that I woeld not recommend it as • farmer or small-holder's general purpose fowl. At the •ime time from • fancier's point of view it is a handsome fowl, and Clle that the writer greatly admires ; even no mach that if I had space and cash it is one of the firm breeds I should adopt for exhibition imposes.</p>
      </div>
