      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-04_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</head>
        <p>FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</p>
        <p>By Philip Snowden. til P</p>
        <p>WILL THE OSBORNE DECISION BE REVERSED THIS SESSION.</p>
        <p>The question which is exciting trade unionists a good deal more than the Veto Bill is that of the Osborne decision. I went through the five or six years when we were agitating for the alteration of the law as it had been laid down in the famous Taff Vale case, and I never found the same amount of interest in that case, and the same•grim determination to have the rights of trade unionists restored, as I meet with every- where among trade unionists and labour men now in regard to the present situaticin. There is a very general feeling that this decision is an attack on the right of the working classes to combine to send their own men to Parliament, and there is no 'surer or quicker way to get up the fight- ing spirit of the British Workman than ' to tell him he shall not do a certain thing.</p>
        <p>AFTER FORTY YEARS. Trade unionists have been engaged in political work for nearly forty years. The oldest member of the present House Of Commons is a trade union member who was elected as an independent Labour man, and all the time he has sat in the House of Commons, up to the House of Lords decision, he did so under the unquestioned impression that it was a legal thing for his trade union to pay for his support. The Labour Party was formed ten years ego. Its basis was the assumption that there could be no question about the legality of a Labour Party, or of the legal right of a trade union to spend its money for political representation. And then,when the party had got well established, when it had got well organised, when it had got a footing in Parliament, when it was beginning to show the value and the possi- bilities of Labour representation, the law steps in and says" You must not do this. It is no pan of the legal powers given to you by the Trade Union Acts to gain your legitimate object by Parliamentary action. You can strike to gain your aims, you can starve yourselves and your children to get better conditions, but you must not adopt the peaceful means of using your votes collec- tively to obtain these reforms." WHAT: WILL HAPPEN.</p>
        <p>It is not in human nature to take a defiance like that lying down, and that I think is the reason why there is such a strong deter- mination among the trade unionists to have the long practised right restored to them. There is no difference of opinion as to what is wanted. Nothing will satisfy but the complete restitution of liberty to use the trade union, with the approval of the majority of the members, for the purpose of securing Labour representation. The question which is waiting an answer is '• What will the Government do? Will they allow the House of Commons to pass the Labour Party's Bill if the majority of members are willing to do so?" I do not pretend to be able to give an answer to that question. I can only examine the probabilities, and on the whole review I should be inclined to say that something will be done this session, but whether it will give the Labour Party all they ask I am not prepared to say. I suppose every legislative measure is a com- promise between the full demand of the one side, the moderate views of the middle, and the opposition of the other side. But I have good reason to think the Bill which will be passed will be one which will not be unsatis- factory, if it does not concede completely everything which the Labour Party demands.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-04_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>PROBABLY A PRIVATE BILL</head>
        <p>PROBABLY A PRIVATE BILL</p>
        <p>I do not think it is likely that the Gov- ernment will take up the question in a Bill of their own. It is more probable that they will leave it to the Labour Party to introduce a Bill. A considerable number of private members have promised to ballot for such a Bill, and if luck should be on the side of the just and deserving a good place will be secured. The debate upon the Bill in that case will take place some Friday afternoon, and its fate on Second Reading will depend upo the attitude the Government take up tow rds it. It is most likely, I should say, up or they will take the course they did on the Trades Disputes question. it will be re embered that the Government introduced a ill which was very unsatisfactory. The - bour party had a good place in the ballot</p>
        <p>iat year, and they put forward a Bill of</p>
        <p>wit' own. The Government left the matter the free decision of the House, with the 'lt that the Labour Party's Bill was on Second Reading, and the Govern- ok this as an instruction to amend II accordingly, which was done. lENDING IN COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>, like that is likely to happen in the Osborne Bill. The Prime ill probably give the Labour</p>
        <p>Bovril</p>
        <p>be from</p>
        <p>taken.</p>
        <p>(See Poster.)</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>Party's Bill a mild blessing, and leave the Second Reading vote to the House, reserving the liberty to take up a hostile attitude to the Bill on its emergence from committee, it is not to the liking of the Government. But this course of action involves that the Government give time for the committee stage of the Bill, if it passes the Second Reading, and about that I think, there is little reason for doubt. The Government could not refuse to give time for the com- mittee stage. The question is so urgent, and risks of alienating Labour support must not, in the present position of the Government be incurred. So that all the probabilities seem to roe to point to the passing of a Bill this session, or at least the passing of a Bill through the House of Commons. What will be its fate in "another place" I am not prepared to prophecy. • THE PREMIER'S STATEMENT. But if the Labour Party's Bill gets into Committee it is certain that there will be a good deal of opposition to the clause which gives power to enforce the compulsory levy. I believe there are a good many members who have reserved their decisions on this point. It will be remembered that the Prime Minister, in his answer to Mr. Barnes on November 22nd, said that the Govern- ment intended to give Payment of Members and official election expenses, if they had a majority after the General Election; and that they would further propose legislation empowering trade unions to include in their objects and organisation the provision of a fund for Parliamentary and municipal action and representation and kindred objects, and to combine for such purposes, provided that the opinion of the union is effectively ascer- tained, and that there shall be no compulsion upon any member to contribute to the fund. VERY UNSATISFACTORY.</p>
        <p>That statement of the Prime Minister's cannot he regarded as at all satisfactory, especially when read with answers that he gave to further questions which were addressed to him. It seemed clear that the meaning of Mr. Asquith's statement wasl that the Bill the Government have in their mind would simply legalise voluntary con- tributions into a voluntary fund ; that the trade union organisation could be legally used to promote Labour representation 'but not one penny of the ordinary funds of the, union may be used for the purpose; nor is, there to be a universal charge in the union contribution for this purpose, with the right , of a dissentient member to claim the repay- ment of his proportion. The fund for Labour 'representation and political action is to be distinctly separate fund, and no member is to be compelled to contribute to it. If this be the scheme of the Government, and it seems clear that it is from the Premier's statement, then one can see no reason why the "opinion of the union should be effec- tively ascertained" as to whether political action shall be one of the objects of the union, except that it will enable those, who are opposed, to vote against other members paying for political representation for the benefit of themselves and the other members of the union. THE DEFINITE DEMAND.</p>
        <p>I think I may venture to say that such a Bill as the Prime Minister outlined will not be satisfactory to the Labour Party. Nothing can be accepted as a just settlement of the case which will leave to a minority of the members of a trade union the legal right to decline to pay towards the political work of a union, when such political work has been made one of the legal objects of the union and incorporated in the rules by the votes of the majority of members. That is the posi- tion the Labour party takes up on this question, and for that they will fight. Some- thing better than Mr. Asquith promised will have to be offered by the Government. A good many members of Parliament are pre- pared to go the whole length of the Labour Party's demand, but they will not go against the Government. The best course, there- fore, appears to be for the Government to leave the question to the free choice of the House Of Commons.</p>
