        <p>"A " team match at Warrington provided a ant display of football and the Warrington lads wonderfully well to bent a combination which did "stain" like T. Llewellyn, Tyrant, B. Dix.. McLean. I make nn hesitation in saying that for the presence d Fish the Warrington men d have lost. The "Irrepressible " looked Pisingly fit, and by his masterly play he o ,m- -ly checkmated the dangerous Oldham right wing. over, he got Warrington the winning point by a of characteristic head-work which gave Board• a try. If it wasn't for his tendency to hang back iad of follow the ball, "Jack" would be as luable as ever. He is still without an equal for try and judgment. Mclntyre played a great e at full back, and his punting was something to eel at. Carter is improving weekly at ceotre. the ',nivel., Lloyd and Daintith, have still the old of kicking too much. Forward, the "A " team Pak, and very few of the juniors seem likely to lop enough bone &amp;IA sinew to qualify for the ner team.</p>
        <p>veral clubs have made havte to strengthen their is in preparation for the Cup tournament. istratioa closed on Tuesday, and since our last the following transfers have taken place as from Salford to Oldham, for .114.50; Hogg from an to Barrow; Winterhurn (Reighley) and W. B. Le (Halifax) to Bradford; and Jolley (Runcorn) to ill. With the exception of Lomas, none of these are of the stamp required by I.Varrington. and it testionable whether the great Salford centre is th such a sum after 14 years hard football. If be v Oldham to win either the League or the Cup rill not be considered dear at Watersbeddings, re they coin money despite the Auociation game.</p>
        <p>o-day's match should be with Hull, who are Iys popular visitors to the Warrington head- rters. At the time of writing, however, the match vs certain to be frozen off unless there is a very d thaw. Possibly an adjournment will he no bad g for Warrington, who have only a weak team lable. Dickenson, Mclntyre and Crevice are all for playing, and the selection on Thursday night</p>
        <p>Iley. full back ; Waywell, Taylor,tneworth, and lshaw, threcinerters; Brooker and Daintith, half Thomas, Johnston,Shugars, Chesters,Skelhorn Polson, forwards.</p>
        <p>reeve, has been to see Mr. Jones, the specialist at wpool, with his damaged knee,and the probabilities that it will be three weeks before he is fit again play. Bcth the player awl the club are to be pethised with in such as unfortunate happening be eve of the Cup-ties.</p>
        <p>It is supercilious to my es you do, that it "would look ridlonlous " to see the touch-judgert sprinting parallel with a centre and wing who were exchanging passes on the run. If it does not " lurk ridicule. "to see the referee racing about the field, I tail to see who one should ridicule the touch-judges for attempting it. Personally, I should like to see more "sprinting" on the pert of the touch-judges, and you may take it from me that the spectators would not object. You bong:rs that if the touch-judge were merely to raise his ties ens hint to the refer. it Nereid confound the pleyers. I don't think no. Rule 15 of the Laws of the Game of the Football Associa- tion states "In the event of a supposed iofrinee- meet of the lewe, the hell shall be in play until decisien hra been gives"; in other words,until the referee shall bare blown his 'titivate. Why not adept It ? The player. must leans to pier the whistle. Just a word or two with reference to yonr inconsistence. In one paragraph NOII Write: It would never dolt. my npiaion. to Itand over such powert to the totie:-jodges. The referee must be sole judge." I egree entirely and have never questioned hi. authority. But what about your neat paragraph. which contains the following contradiction : "If the hooch-judge intervenes for ohetrucr ion or rough play, then it should he binding upon the referee to tee that the proper penalty is imposed. That in clout the only amendment to the rule, se •t. prerant existing. which I think would be • reed advantage." Now, sir, perhaps you will show ore how the referee can le the sole judge of the game under these circum. stance.; end why, if the touch-judge In competent to Intervene for obstruction or rough pine, he is not capable of selling attention to oil-mite pin.— without the rink of" looking ridiculous." In conclusion, If the touch-judges were callari upon to easier, the referee in carrying out the game more than they are, it would make the players" play the game" it would train the touch- judges by giving them practical experience to become better referee.; sort it would glee the spectators lees cause to exprese their diesatisfaction with the conduct of the garee. At leant that is the humble opinion of, Yonee kr- EDMUND OSBORNE.</p>
