        <p>MR. OSBORNE AND THE POWERS OF TOUCH JUDGES. Jr. Edmund Osborne has favoured me with a titer elaboration of his views respecting the duties ouch judge., and a criticism of coy remarks Mat I am still of opinion that with competent I Preen, and touch judges who will exercise their rem in regard to obstruction, the game will be veil and ruled as it ought to be. The quality of erring is not high. The sooner the Union realises fact awl takes remedial measures, the better it I be. However there is room for more than one :lion on Mr. Oshorne's suggestions, which are nted herewith :</p>
        <p>Dear ' Ovalist,'—lt is good of you to call atten- tion to my letter, or rather a portion of it, which I addreeaed to' Veteran" of the "Daily Dispatch" last week, •nrl to entities, the amendments to the laws nf the Northern Union game wbioh I sug- gested therein. That you do not agree with there, I no rether, surprised : I el.ould have thought an enthusiast like yotraelf would have teen delighted to pooh them forwent. As it In evident thee opinionn are somewhat divided, I shell be grateful if you will allow me to rely a hurried word or two as to my own views end to add a little friendly criticism of yoors. Now, to, contention is that the tonch-judgee are, or shoold be competent to call attention to *erten Infringements which mar hive escaped the notice of the referee and that thsir aer•ices should be requieitioned. I have tot suggested that the referee should be soperseded because I believe that be should be the mole judge. Assistance does not wacoesarily wean control, end you barn confounded Co two,l think.</p>
