      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-04_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>_ _ _ Of •1 Ihunleis awl% I</head>
        <p>_ _ _ Of •1 Ihunleis awl% I</p>
        <p>--- - - - IRISH AFFAIRS. NOTES FOR 1-111 S-H READERS.</p>
        <p>A PARLIAMENT MUCH MEANS BUSINESS Although the work of the new Parliament will conxist for the next few days of the mere fel-moldy of " swearing in " the members, the gathering of the combatanta on the scene of the coming fight quickens interest even now in the momentous sendn upon which RP are entering. All shades of political opinion, as expteseed in the various journals, agree at any rate in the belief that we are about to enter upon one of the biggest fights which the neople of this country have witsesssd on the supremely interesting point of the constitution. It appears to he more than probable that the Parliament Bill will be proc,eded with to the exclusion of all other subjects which do not compel attention. Earlier on we had loud demands mode for a truce in the year of Coronation and it was protested vehemently that all " contentions legislation " was to be kept bock until the King had , been crowned. The meek proposal by these stout defenders of the King. who seems to have taken to themeelvet the special care of the royal person, han come down to a suggest ion that no roam,' of combat should lie beard in the months of June and Jely. The modesty of the later proposal sungests that the Unionists perceive clearly enough that the Government with the men behind them mean business. The same political powers that called for a truce here are demanding what they are pleased to tetni a " suopensioe of hoetilitiee " in Ireland in recognition of the Royal visit in July next. Thane awful people the Nationalists are commanded by the , superior person of the l•ni mist guide to cease their wranulings and while Unionists in Ireland are preen- ing the point that the visit of the King is entirely non-political the' Times " hits out with the smashing opinion that the Royal visit cannot be diesoviated ' from the political conattions of the time. "It must be taken," we are told by this oracle, " as an indi- cation that a Borne Rule Bill will not he introduced this year, since it would be impossible for his Majesty ,to visit Dublin while Leland was in the midst of a hot dissension which such a measure cannot fail to excite." This policy of calling for peace in one breath aril hurling threats in the next will lie treated with the contempt it deserves. : It is really too transparent and in Ireland at any rate 'it will deceive no one. We have bad rather too much of the "wolf-sheep" in the past to be disquieted at his re-appearance now. Long before the historic 1 pageant coulee on these people who ate so anxious for the good rind the peace of Ireland will have bird the opportunity f abolishing once awl for all the central force which has so thug dieurlied that bl-underatuod country's peace. TARIFFS ABROAD AND FOOD AT HOME. A mutter of considerable interest to English and Irish alike is that of the proposed tariff agreement between Canaria and the United States. The pro- posal in brief aims at the dropping of the tariff on all food articles sent hy Canada into the States. In the past because of this tariff the Canadian farmer 'Mend it more profitable bread his or-since not scram a ferry to the ceiehhourittg States, but across the Ocean to distant England. Now, Amu' i this tariff' barrier be removed it is inevitable that thee wheat, dairy produce, vegetables, eggs, poultry, cattle and sheep and all the other food attic'e's which by the aid of the refrigerator Canada poured in abundance into the English marliet must go mole end more to America and lees to England. For the English eon- earner we see no refuge from a rise in prices which moot follow the diversion of an hugs • food, supply unless it be in the quick and ecientifie cultivation of the granary and garden at the heart of the Empire, which is to be I and in Ireland. Before • week is out we shall know better what chance this turd! agreement has of adoption. America etande to gain considerably. President Taft is strongly in favour of it—it is in fact his measure, and Ins urgent recom- mendation in his Message to Congress At prevent there appears to be no ground for Canada's failing to ratify the agreement since it gives her the near and immense market which she has so long desired. 'rho wheel of international affair, moves with rapid and sometimes revolutionary turns and we know there are minds in Ireland which will not be slow to appreciate the deep interest of the issue we have merely outlined. The question, whether it is translated into legislative act or '- opportune tor thinking men in It land to consider suggesting an it doe a food store for England and • land to produce to the height of its n. resources, almost self-supprting lowest estimate, for Ireland. THE APPEAL OF THE NATIONAL T' which wan Mend this week is one whi will have the approval and the generu every Nationalist both here and in abundantly clear that if full ads ante,: of the Irish cause to-day is to be t charge, it is ienperative that every must be up arid doing. Dilatorinese near sight, the nearness of whit opponents opsnly affirm, is of come will be noted that 00 SUppOr t is apki great drain on nor resources whicl an the two election, of last year. I of the great demands of cur Ere to-morrow that support of the is sought. Not a member can Westinineter ; a Home Rule ci Britain, which we who live in Ore well in abeolutely imperative. it Registration calls out on every literature must be scattered brut organisation must be extended, navy point, and no man who imagines this can be done wit Swift's appeal on the occasion o COMM to our mini PS explained that giving in this form all Christiana held at extraordir and the two-minute sermon clo exhortation "Down with the dust Irishman to-day will fail to apt reference to the appeal cf the Nati</p>
        <p>latural t') pt</p>
