      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-04_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SAIVIDAY, FEBUARY 4. 1911.</p>
        <p>Next week Parliament COMING TO enters on the business GRIPS. of the new Session. This week it has been engaged in formalities—the re-election of the SPEAKER, and the taking of the oaths. An amusing but rather disconcerting interlude was furnished by Mr. GINNELL, who pro- tested that the compliments paid to the SPEAKER were insincere. A large number of members, it appeared, had a serious griev- ance, although they had not the courage to proclaim it. When the nature of this grievance was disclosed, the House was con- vulsed with laughter. It was the grievance of the man who could not catch the SPEAKER'S eye. This quaint outbreak rather added to than detracted from the harmony of the proceedings. The House of Commons, whatever superior persons of Lord Cuazox's type may believe, has a profound sense of the dignity of the oldest and greatest representative Chamber in the world. The dignity of the House of Commons and the dignity of the Chair are indissolubly joined together as the thing symbolised and the symbol. And it has been the:good fortune but the deserved good fortune, of the House of Commons that, with scarcely an excep- tion, the men who have sat in the Speaker's Chair have preserved intact the highest traditions of impartiality, courage and courtesy. It may be said, with truth—to adapt a well-known epigram—that legisla- tive Chambers get the kind of Speaker which they deserve. And to say that is to pay the I highest possible compliment to the House of , Commons.</p>
        <p>The coming week will be occupied in dis- cussion over a very wide field. The debate upon the Address is intended to give scope for the consideration of any subject of national importance. But the political situ- ation is necessarily such that the average man cannot feel that Parliament has " come to grips" with the really vital issue until the Parliament Bill is reached. It was on the Parliament Bill that the General Election was fought. To all other questions the attitude of the majority of the electors was simply this: "Some of us may be eager for some measure for which others of us are no so enthusiastic, some of us may even be</p>
        <p>e:yided as to the desirability of certain measure.; but we all agreed that the pass- ing of the Parliament Bill would be such a good thing that we are ready to sink all differences for that end."</p>
        <p>We wonder how the AN ALULCUED FABLE "Daily News" of FROM WARRINGTON. yesterday comes to publish the following</p>
        <p>remarkable story: There appears to be in circulation at Warring.. On a fable, seemingly believed in by a good mane, that the German Navy possesses a gun capable of throwing. pro*tile two miles farther than the most posetful piece of ordnance in the 13r1tish Navy. Consequently, no the story goes, oar ships could all be sunk by the Germans before they came within striking distance. A correspondent having called the attention of Lord Fisher to this latest variation of the Navy scale, received the following reply: "Kilverstone Hall, Thetford, Norfolk." " Lord Fisher begs to think Mr. Fildes for his kind letter. The gentleman who told him shoos the German gun most be a fool." Mr. Fildes has since written us to say that "As the rumour I heard of appears to , come from Warrington it may interest you,"' Well, we have never heard of either Mr. Fildes or the rumour before. It seems a curious sort of fable to originate in Warring- ton, which has not, so far as we know, any special interest in German naval artillery. Possibly some nervous National Service Leaguer has dreamed an evil dream— though we hesitate to utter such s thought without substantiation. However, Lord Fisher seems to have a very proper and statesmanlike estimate of the individual who may be responsible for the rumour. But it seems hard that poor old Warrington should be publicly credited with harbouring the fool in question?</p>
        <p>The proposal of Mr THE BENEFITS (?) Holman Kingdon for OF ALL-BRMSH an All-British SHOPPING. Shopping Week in</p>
        <p>WtGringto-n-- was brought before the annual meeting of the Warrington Chamber of Commerce, on Monday, and although several of the ' members expressed doubt respecting the wisdom of the suggestion, it was so far approved as to be referred to the Trademen's Association whose members are primarily interested. Mr. Kingdon claims that the proposal affords a common meeting ground for Free Traders and Protectionists. That depends on what is implied in the suggestion. If it is meant that we are to be asked to buy an inferior home-made article then buyers will all object. If it is only intended to bring before the peoplo the merits of the home production then little need be said against it. But is not this being done every week of the year? The merits of an article, whether of home or foreign origin, are the constant prime con- sideration of both the shopkeeper who buys to retail, and the public who purchase. If the home-made goods are inferior, is it not the best way to emphasize this inferiority by buying' the foreign-made article, so that the home manufacturer will be obliged to make his product equal to the imported article ? Is it any permanent benefit to the manufacturing trade of England to buy what it produces whether the best on the market or not? But" says the All-British Shopping enthusiast, "they are generally superior." Possibly, but the buyer must be allowed to be the judge, as he provides the cash.</p>
        <p>Then thcre is the powerful argument sug- gested by Mr. Frost in the question "Is not an All-British Week likely to suggest to the foreigner the idea that he should inaugurate a similar form of mild boycott of British goods by an All-French or All-German Week r• And inasmuch as we export double the amount of manufactures we import, is their power of retaliation not double our own? The probability is that we, as "a nation of shop-keepers" stand to lose much more than we can gain by the general adop- tion of an All-British Shopping Week. Though the idea has a nice imperial flavour about it, reflection leads us to the conclusion that it savours of a narrow outlook. We find, on inquiry, that the suggest:on does not meet with much favour among many of the leading tradesmen. They foresee a difficulty in differ- entiating among certain classes of goods— English millinery with French feathers for instance—and in other trades the public demand for well-known foreign goods is so great that the British articles could not be "pushed "without an,injury to that freedom of trade which we all enjoy. There is a pretty strong general opinion that "the game is not worth the candle"!</p>
        <p>The reciprocity agree- THE CANADIAN ment between the ARGUMENT. United States and</p>
        <p>Canada has struck another nail—probably the last nail neces- sary—into the coffin of Imperial Preference. If we put a duty on American corn, the American exporter would send his grain into this country via Canada. But, although the Tariff Reformers are determined to punish, if they possibly can, the Free Trade Govern- ment for the breakdown of Tariff Reform, the more sober-minded of them admit that geographical conditions cannot easily be tranformed or nullified by the most " scientific " tariff ever devised, and that, at no time and under any circumstances would Canada be likely to refuse the terms that the United States have offered.</p>
        <p>The Western Farmers A STEP TOWARDS of Canada have declared FREE TRADE, in the most unambiguous and emphatic manner that they do not want the Preference which the Tariff Reformers in this country have denounced the Government for not offering to them. What they wanted was freer trade in Canada, not a restriction of trade in Great Britain to give them an artificial advantage.. The reciprocity agreement between the United States and Canada is a step which natural conditions indicate as rational and almost inevitable. But Tariff Reformers in this country think that they can annihilate geographical conditions and divert the natural tendency of trade into artificial channels with the greatest of ease. We have gained so much by Free Trade our- selves, while our competitors have been hampered by Protection, that for purely selfish reasons we might regret any move- ment towards Free Trade in competing countries. As, however,it is one of our own Colonies that will benefit, we need not complain.</p>
        <p>The Pretoria Colliery Relief Food. Ott Tuesday evenmg the Mayor of Warrington f Couocillor Dr. Joseph attended a meeting of the Central Relief Committee at Bolton. to consider the suggested amalgamation of the fundu frr the relief of the sufferers I.y the dieaster et the Pretora Colliery. 11e scheme for the amalgamation if ,he funds was approved, and representatives were •ppoinied to the General Committee for the administramo of the Feed.</p>
        <p>• CO•4I.7LINC: Tnwspareet Comsat for (.1.1., Ciao* tr.c. 6d., and le , poet f reo.—Adot.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-04_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>There may yet be a lake in Victoria Park. The Borough Surveyor has been instructed to report upon the cost. Now then, all ye open-air bathers, make yourselves heard, or the chance will be gone for this generation, because the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee has the matter in hand.</p>
        <p>The Museum Committee has purchased the oil painting "The Rescue" by Frank Brangwyn A.R. A. for the sum of £55. When they talk,' of their Raphaels, Corregios awl stuff, He shifted his trumpet, and only took snuff." That would be Councillor Pemberton, doubtless !</p>
        <p>On the anniversary (Monday) of the death of Charles 1., it may be recalled that the death warrant of the King is preserved in the Library of the House of Lords. The signatures are in some cases as clear and distinct as if they had only just been written. In others they are very faint and almost indistinguishable, and in others, again, efforts have been made with more or less success to erase the names. • •</p>
        <p>• , • The peerage contains many pitfalls of nomenclature. For instance, there is a Lord Camden and a Lord Campden ; a Lord Desart and a Lord Dysart; a Lord Langford and a Lord Longford ; a Lord Sudley and a Lord Sudeley ; a Lord Middleton, and a Lord Midleton ; Lord Carrington has brothers who spell their name with one " r," and are authorised to do so by Royal licence. • • Knutsford-road Swing Bridge is, after all, going to be again closed to the public while repairs are made to the hydraulic cylinder. The General Purposes Committee has agreed to the Canal Co.'s application. Latchford residents are expressing themselves strongly on the matter. They contend that the Company should provide vehicles which will convey the public from one side to the other cia the Cantilever Bridge.</p>
        <p>• Burnley Corporation is another of those which are coming into line with up-to-date municipalities, and setting an example to Warrington. The Tramways Committee there have decided to erect a shelter and waiting-room at the junction of two import- ant car routes at an estimated cost of £270. Meanwhile Warrington passengers are ,-xposed to the wind and rain. They suffer. But not in silence ! • • . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Holman Kingd-on made at least one good point in his advocacy of an All-British Shopping week. Said he " his great bete noir was the man who bought the foreign motor-car. He did not hesitate to say that was a disgraceful thing, m.d the man who usually did it was a Conservative and Pro- tectionist." We unhesitatingly say " hear, hear" to such sentiments. There were many advocates of taxing the foreigner' at the last election who had taken good care to buy a foreign motor-car while there was yet no tax upon it. Tariff Reform, like charity, should begin at home. The late Mr. Lockwood Kipling figures in at least one good story in connexion with his famous son. When Rudyard was twelve he went on a sea voyage with his paternal relative. The elder Kipling became very sea-sick, and went below, leaving the youngster to himself. Presently there was a great commotion overhead, and one of the ship's officers rushed down and banged at Mr. Kipling's door. "Mr. Kipling," he called out, " your boy has crawled out on the yard- arm, and if he lets go he'll drown." "Yes," said Mr. Kipling, glad to know that nothing serious was the matter; "but he wont let go."</p>
        <p>• What about the washing? The Streets Improvements Committee has passed a resolution which will cause some heart- burning among the tenants of houses on the west side of Wilderspool Causeway. The resolution is as follows:—" That notices be served upon the owners and occupiers of premises on the west side of .Wilderspool Causeway to remove the posts and stumps erected on the river bank." This is a famous drying ground on washing day, and the housewives of Wilderspool will be rarely wrath if they are driven into their small backyards. There is an opportunity here for one of the Councillors of the Ward to earn undying,fame and gratitude by chivalrously championing the cause of the ladies. • •</p>
        <p>Warrington Guardians seem to do deputa- tion work in style. For instance, three Guardians and the Master, recently visited Ashton and Oldham on a deputation. The Guardians—as befits unpaid public servants with a dignity to uphold—travelled first class, and the Master was accommodated in the humble " third." The Rev. A. M. Mitchell's democratic soul abhors such narrow class distinctions, and he lodged a protest against Guardians having first-class fare and expenses. He thought third-class was good enough even for Bishops now-a- days. But the Guardians thought otherwise and the rev, gentleman was as usual, in a distinguished minority. We wonder if these high and mighty Bumbles ever condescend to walk the platform with the humbleofficials or do the latter follow at a respectful dis- tance, and maybe carry a bag or two?</p>
        <p>A Reuter message tells us that the famous Hope diamond has been bought for £60,000 by an American as a present for his wife. The "Times '• comment on this is as follows:— It is questionable whether any romance ever woven around the subject of precious stones can rival the liktory of the flops diamond, with its long series of murders, suicides, and other mis- fortunes. Its first recorded purchase was in the latter half of the seventeeth century. Among Ili owners have been Mine. de Montespan (disgraced), )(alio Antoinette (guillotined), and Salina Zubayba, the favourite of Abdul Humid (shot deed). Another one-time possessor is May Yohe, who has passed through many vicissitudes since she was the wife of Lord Francis Hope. She is credited with stating that she would not wear the stone again if it was given to her with £l,OOO a year. It is to be hoped that the new owner has more courage than superstition! • _</p>
        <p>The news of the fire at Messrs. Richmond's works caused much perturbation to Alder- man Bennett, for there was a prospect of another local landmark, around which hovez many happy recollections, being demolished. Part of the premises now used as offices by the firm was in days long gone by the residence of Dr. Priestley, the world-famous tutor at the old Warrington Academy, and a tablet recording this interesting bit of local history was placed on the building a few years ago by the Warrington Society. Imagine the worthy alderman's alarm • on realizing the dread possibility that the doctor's house might soon be a heap of ruins ! Hastily he made his way to the scene and was obviously greatly relieved on finding that the building</p>
        <p>—and the tablet—had survived the outbreak and that another relic of" old Warrington " was preserved for the inspiration of patriotic townsmen.</p>
        <p>In our correspondence columns we publish a timely appeal from Mr. John P. Reynolds, hon. treasurer of the Warrington Musical Society, for increased support of the excellent educational work which the Society is doing in our midst. The fact that in a large town like Warrington there are only 170 subscri- bers to the concerts is cestainly not to the credit of the music-loving public, and we hope the letter will enlist the practical help of all who care for the maintenance of a high class- ical standard in our local musical life.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-04_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>LOCAL PARS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARS.</p>
        <p>Meeting of Conservatl in Agents. Mr. lferl,ert Hayes Warrington) and Mr. J. 11. Bottomley (Wigan) were present at the annual meet- ing of the North of England Conservative Agents' Association held on Monday in the rooms of the Constitutional Association, Liverpool. Personal. . .</p>
        <p>• Miss Margery ladding, daughter of Rev. T. ;bidding, radgate, has passed the t,:enior Cambridge Local Examivatton in the subjects required for exemption from the Matriculation examination of the L'niveraity of London.</p>
