        <p>the Kingdom.</p>
        <p>The Warringtnn Rural Dodnet Council was held in Rewsey Chambers, on Wednesday afternoon, Mr. George Mather (vice-chairman) presiding. There were present—the Rev. A. M. Mitchell. Dr. Murray, Messrs. IV. Houghton, J. W. Knowles, P. I.ongton, A. Brooks. IV. Farness, J. C. Sutton (clerk), B. S. New- combe (surveyor), H. H. Homage (assistant surveyor), F. Kerfoot (inspector), Dr.Sephton (medical officer of health). The Medical Officer reported that during the month ended February lot there had been 2S deaths on the district of those tO were male and 8 female. There had been Di deaths ; 9 male and 7 female. The death rate from all causes was eimal to 14;) per thousand against :41 for the corresi ouding period last year. Only one child under one year if age had died, but five wets over Or. They had had the lowest death-rate dur,ng the past quarter in any rural district in Eagle.</p>
        <p>Hearts of Oak Benefit Society. The annual meeting of the local Electoral and Medical Aid Branches of the above society was held on Monday evening last in St. Paul's schoolroom, Kendrick-street, before a goof attendance of member* The respective secretaries submitted their report with the statement of accounts for the year, and the mem- bers had cause to congratulate themselves on the satisfactory results of the year's working. An amalgamation scheme suggeNted by the Joint Com- mittees was considered; and after a lengthy discussion it was unanimously decided that the two Societies combine. The next quarterly meeting will be held at the Winmarleigh ('ale, Sankey-street, on April 24th. 'rhe officers nporioted for the :rear were :—Chairman, Bro. A. C. Binks, viee-chairrnan. Bre. J. Italie, treasurer, Bro. J. W. Hall, financial secretary, lieu. Legge, and organising secretary, Bro. W. Leach, 4, Ilawthorne-terrace, Stockton Beath, who will be pleased to furnish to intending members any particulars they may re.mire.</p>
        <p>ANTHRACITE STOVE 6, cheaper then coal or gos once filled burns 24 hours without furtheratternion for lld., no smoke, fumes or soot. In/section. IS, GARLAND, 51, 53, &amp; 55, Itri.ige at., Warrington. Stove may he teen burning dAily.—Advt. The Clerks' Union.</p>
        <p>_ The IA arrington Branch of the National nion of !Clerks continues to make steady progress since the public meeting on November 18th. At the request of the Members, the Secretary wrote to Mr. Harold Smith, M.P. asking whether he would be prepared to support 0 bill to be introduced shortly into Parlia- ment of which the following will be the most impor- tant points. (I) The Inspection of Micen under the Factory and Workshop Act ; {2l The inclusion of clerks under the fair wage clause of government contracts ; (3) The adoption of a minimum wage for clerks employed by the Government vie , Ilss. per week for all clerks over 21 years of age livinc in London and provincial towns, and 275. (Id. per week fur those living in rural districts. The following is an extract from the Member's letter : My dear Sir,— lam obliged by your letter of yesterday's date. Von know that I have much sympatily with the objects of your Union, and I can assure you that when the measures yon outline are introduced in the House, I i shall be n may place and watch your interest with close attention. —Yours truly, HlllOl.O SITMI. Mr. A. 11. Croafield has further shown the deep interest which he takes in the Braneh, by becoming an hon- orary member of and subscriber to it.</p>
        <p>Royal Present to a Ilarrlngtonlan. Mr. James it. W. Comber, late of Warrington. has been the recipient of a mark of favour from 11.1111. the Duke of Connaeght on the occasion of His Royal Ilighneases emit to the oldest British colony of Sierra Leone in December last, the Royal party, consiming of the Duke, the Duchess, Princess Patricia. and the ladies and gentlemen forming their suites, number- ing about thirty-six, made a trip up the mountain rail- way of the Sierra Leone Government Railway Com- pany, under the personal conduct of the general manager. Mr. James R. W. Comber (whose home is at Lower Walton, and who was formerly in the office of the Great Western Railway at Bank Quay). The trip was made in the royal saloon carriages which were decorated for the occasion by the members of the rail- way staff, in order to see the fine view of the river, town, and sea from the terrace garden of Mr. Comber's bungalow. At the end of the trip the Duke personally thanked Mr. Comber for his kindness, and said he had enjoyed it very much. On the arrival of the next mail steamer, on January 11th, after the Duke's return to England. Mr. Comber received a pair of gold sleeve links with the imperial crown and royal initials A.W. in gold and green enamel, accom- panied by a letter signed by the Duke's everry to the following effect —Presented by H.Rdl. the Duke of Connaught as a memento of his visit to Sierra Leone when he took a trip up the mountain railway.</p>
        <p>I Van of Sousa's Bast' : Our readers are reminded that Mr. J. P. Sonsa and liiii famous band will gives concert at the l'alace and Hippodrome on Monday afternoon next ar 2.3 d. The programme will consist of the masterpieces of the German, French, English, Russian. Italian and American schools, and of all other IiSIIOIIII. Some of .t.lo most popular of the Sousa compositions will also be played. The soloists who will appear are Miss Virgins Root (soprano), Misn Nicoline Zedeler Odolinstl, and Mr. Herbert Is Clarke 'solo cornet).</p>
        <p>Peony Popular Concerts. The popularity of the weekly concerts in Bold-street Wesleyan Schoolroom has been well maintained this , reason, and on Saturday evening there was another large audience to Satan to the high e'ass programme submitted by Mr. Harry Berrev's partY of talented artistes from Wid nee. The items were well chosen, admirably rendered, and appealed to humour an well as setdiment. Mr. Berrey is to be congratulated upon arranging an entertainment which was generally voted to be one of the best on record.</p>
        <p>Next Week at the Palace.. Fred Karno's company in In amusing sketch "The football match " will he the chief attraction at the Palace next week. Other turns are: untie Archer, mimic ; Snowball and Dolia. dancers; The quality ,inattette in "Gems of meal harmony"; Captain Kettle, coin and card comedian Ada Gatley, in her pot.pourri speciality; and Haley's juveniles in a carnival scene, including " Dot " and "Spot," the emallest Miettist before the public. Perfection pictures will ale° lie shown. _ _ .</p>
