        <p>On Monday evening last Si the above Lodge A paper was given by Pro 'Sewall. Dealing particu- larly with the Wem district of Shropshire, the lecturer took his hearers along with him and his vivid description and excellent illustrations created grAt interest. On the proposition of P G Walter Dennett, sei,nded by P G W. Webster, and supported by PPOM W. Holbrook, a very hearty vote of thanks was accorded the essayist for his educative address. Songs were els° given during the evening by OW. Webster. PG W. Dennett, and V 0 A. Hare, whilst P G W. Barnes accompanied with his usual ability. Dr. Barnardo's Homes.</p>
        <p>The annual Inot opening in connection with the Warrington Habitation the Young Helpers' Leagne wan held in the Cairo-street roans on Saturday. It was a very successful meeting, about 65 being present. The proceeding. opened with tea, after which Mins Carden. the Warden for Lancashire, gar, an address on the life and work of the late Dr. Bernardo. Some interesting lantern alidea were shown of the carious homes. A abort entertainment followed, to which the pupils or Stockton L.das School contributed come pretty dances. —Owing to the unavoidable absence of Mrs. Rylands (the president), Mr. C. Jackson Holmes wan in the chair, end spoke a few werds to the children on the objects of the League. It is pleasing to find that the membership hats largely increased during the past year. The collection 1.1 the boxes together with subscripticmt, amounted to over shooting Accident at Bewsey. " " .</p>
        <p>Is Monday afternoon the Ilewse; flail estate was the scene of a shooting accident, resulting in serione injuries to Mr. Percy E. Cleaver, Devonshire House, Devonshire-road, Liverpool. It appears that Mr. Cleaver had rented the shooting rights over the estate from the beginning of the sees, and on Monday he was ' paying his final visit. Mr. Cleaver has the reputation of being a very careful shot. Along itiob a . meeting of friends he was engaged (no covert near Bewsey Old Ilall towarde four o'clock in the after- noon. The party temporat ilv separated, and a short time later Mr. Cleaver was discovered lying on the ground having been ahot in the fare. The injured man was carried to the high road on a gate, and a cab having been obtained, he was conveyed to the Warrington Infirmary, where he now lies suffering from serious injuries to the jaw. It is assumed that Mr. Cleaver had placed his loaded gun under his arm elide he lit a cigar. In stooping f ,rward he allowed the gun t, fall and the contents wele discharged in his face. Academy-street Adult School. "</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Aesderny-atreet Adult schrol last week end, was a most eventful one. Several meetings, business. social and religious, were held. and each was well attended.—Ott Saturday evening, a serial programme was gone through, including an unlimited Amount of musical talent ; Messrs. L. Richardson, H. Rigby, Elmetl, Ilis,es Cross and Mr.. C. T. Cox contributing, while honour- able mention must be made of the admirable bass : solo by Mr. Attwell, the comic songs by Mr. Sam Aukland, and the delightful pianoforte solos hr Master Banner. The catering was excellently carried out by members of the school.—On Sunday morning a most intereatinglecturette was given by !dr. Davey Smith. on the Apostle Paul's teaching in metaphor. He showed how through an intimate knowledge of the athletic side of the life of both Greeks and Romana, St. Paul had illustrated many points in hie letters. by a ready use of language characteristic of the soldier, the wreitler and the runner.—ln the afternoon the Rev. W. Reid gave an excellent address on " City Life ; and in the evening, Councillor Wilson, of Birkenhead, referred to tile aims and possibilities of the Adult School movement, when also two exceed-</p>
        <p>inaly well rendered sacred eel. were Riven by Mica Plinaton and Mr. R. Ilawthorne, respectively-.</p>
        <p>Death of In. John S. Green. Many of our readers will regret to hear of the death of Mrs. John S. Green which took place it her residence " Westwood" Park-road, Wallington, Surrey, on Tuesday morning, from complication.. The deceased lady was the fourth daughter of the late Mr. Alfred Few, of Stockton Heath, whose death as the oldest resident we chronicled</p>
        <p>in December last, and she married Mr. John S. Green who formerly carried on the business of a bookseller and stationer, at 31, Bridge. Street, Warrington, a building that has disappeared through the progress of events. Latterly the family removed to London, Ind have lived for Feveral years</p>
        <p>in Kent, recently removing into Surrey on account of the deceasted'a lady's state of health. She was of a genial disposition, and her kindly sympathy endeared her to a large circle of friends and acquaintances, both North and South, who have condoled with the bereaved husband in hie great lose. The funeral took place on Thursday afternoon, at Bandon lull Cemetery. Beddington, Surrey. and was conducted by the Lev. G. E. Darlaston, MA., of the Congregational Church in the Grove. Sytienham, with which the family were until recently identified.</p>
