        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Colon Atonement Commlttee was held at the Workholime 3esterday morning, Mr J Billinge preaiding. There I were also present ; lies T It Matthews, Mowers G Mather, C I. Bent, A .1 Jolley, A Brooks, W C Brooks, W Furriers, A liottornleT (clerk), It It Meacock (valuer) and W II Woodcookfawsistent overseer for Warrington). The Vsluer reported that he lad completed his velum. Aims of several licented hon.* In Warrington and recommended that the naaessmonta he fixed an lot Ion: Patten Arme Hotel, redeced from £390 gross, £3ll 10s rateable to £376 gross, £319 TO, retewllle ; Baths Hotel, reduced from £I4O gross. £ll9 mtgrable 0 EVA gross, £lO2 rateable ; Old Coffee hloo,e, redoeed from £93 gross, .CBl rateable to £.BO gross, Lbtt nstekble ; White Hart Hotel raised from £9O gross, £76 lOs rateable to £l2O gross. Lief: rateasha. 'I ne committee acceptod the figurer, but in tier.., of the What, Hurt Hotel A was agreed that they should not come into totes for the current rate. Mr. If. !letters (Messrs. Hobert Goyim end Co.) who appeared for Messrs. (treenail, Whitley art ( Cll. in the ease of the Patten Arnie Hotel, on Moog acquainted with the new aasessment, maid the committee had treated his clients less favourably then the general body ofTireesed victualler..</p>
        <p>The Chairman: I thought these figurn were of such a character that you would think we had dealt Lenerously with you. Mr. Hatton mid the new licence duty which his client• bad to par in respect of the hotel represented en increa•e of EI.:A 'the reel question for the com• mittee to consider wee the rental the hotel commanded and he did at know that he could pot hi, case !mire strongly than to my that they would lie prepared to let it ton suitable tenant Eta much leas rent than swag represented by the new assessment. The house had made a heavy loss during the whole of the time bia clients had owned it The figuree had been produosd to the valuer net he did not think it could be stinger ed that the loss was thr,ngh had or ostrsvagant mai:eget:tent. The truth was that there did not appear to be a demand in a town like Warrington for no establishment of that size. He contended that it would be imposailile to get any tenant to take the holm to it was at 1.:376 a year. Hie idea was Mut at the very utmost it shoeld be /300 mos. To al:lesser to the chairman, NI.. Hatton mid the betel had been altered considerelily since hie client, became owners, with a view to making it !woe profitable, but instead of profit theta Wall a heavy lose. The licence dory Or. 105 ILS against £4O before the passing of the Finance Act.. . .</p>
        <p>The committee decided to grant n concussion for the correct rate en the tome tennh as had been allowed to other licence., to mis.t the duties imposed ho the Finance Act, but the figures en recommended by the valuer would apply to the new rote. A virnilvr decision was arrived at with regard to the White Hart Hotel, making the figures for the current rate .fibo gross ed ritteahle. Mr. A. Brolv..e appeared for the licence,. of the Baths Hotel and Mr. C.ll. navvorth for the liceocees of the Old Coffee Home end White Hart Hotel. . .</p>
        <p>i The committee pproved the following revised assessments to meet the new conditions entailed by the Finance Act :—Royal Awns rerliteed from £lO gross, £34 rateable t, .1:33 gross, £79 rateable : Sloop Inn front VW gross, £76 IPs. rateable to £79 gross, £67 , rateable ; Iforse sod Jockey, Martinactoft, preeent figures of LAO gross, £34 ratreble to stsnd ; Farmer's Arms, Feernhemil, present ligures of £4O grow, £34 mumble ; Longford Hotel, twinned from £llO gross, £3l 10s, rateable to Lill gross, £5l rateable.</p>
        <p>Welsh Literary Society. rnder the auspices of the above sr eiety a epecial lecture was delivered on Tuesday evening at the (roefield-street Welsh I "Impel. The lecture, .lefiveted in Welsh, was on "Ten, o Nant," by the Rev. 1). It. Williams. minister of Moan Side Welsh Chapel, Manchester. The chair was taken by Mr. W. T. .illiams, JP. who referred in his introductory marks to the prominc.ce to-day of the Welsh tinge and literature in the schools of Wales and in foreign rniversitiew fiscal a) mentioned that ecturer received the National Welsh Eisteddfod on the euhject of his lecture. The lecturer cl his lecture by reviewing the cAulition of k in the loth century, and the three great le th,,t were at %vote in religious, political and circles, and tine influences which these d on the nroluctirm and aspirations of the !I. of his lecure. Mr. Williams throughout are clearly showed that Twin o' Nant was a ml reformer and through his seven ioter- d others which are extinct, he was the means gab at a reform in the social conditions of The character was associated in his ideals ' it Samuel Butler in hie Iludibras than with net clorparis, n of him with Shakespeare. thanks .2.1 propeeed to the lecturer by Mr. se, of Stockton Heath. and seconded by Mr. .of the Revenue Office. Mrs. W.T. Williams Velsh solo during the reciting, and the meet- tided with the singing of the Welsh National</p>
