        <p>The l'Agsluccr, in his adieus, to the Chamber, gold !hot doring the period of acute political controversy from which they had just emerged. the year 1910 wee net infrequently described in the Press or ore the plat- form as "boom' year. Such • descrlptlon esvoured eontesvhat or exaggeration. In this country it was On the whole •ye.r of good trade. The revival which net in demote the second hull Of 19(19 continued steadily and Up to the present tt too had shown ensign of Ateoement. Of thi• there were men, proofs. In the flrst place the elwariog Hofer, worst- shafvel an inc ........ £l,lOOl millions over 1009 That this woe not all due to act icity in legitimate trading might he admitted on it was in part owing to speculation during the robber and lii bonne on the stock exchange, hut making dire aI'OWAI3OO tor that, the Increase was such as to clearly the healthy end vigorotte state of the trade of the country. 'lke Itosrd of Toole returns showed an increase of slightly over £:(0,lar0.000 both in the •spori a to and imports Irons foreign coontries and British t 'Armies. [hooch itt that case it should be borne in mind that the increase was partly acctionted for by the hist... values ol conimodi- notehly in the rase or cotton. Further proof of satisfactory internal snide we. Men found in the better railway return., and, what wee still more feessuring, the atatistics of unemployment revealed an improve- ment in the labour market., the proportion of members of trade onions unemployed being 4.0 per cent. against. o.:f per cent a year previously. The year's business falloff* showed A marked decrease on 1907, sod the returns of peoperiarn filar show a more satisfactory state of things. 1 ~,,, king at all tbose indications. it might safely he said that the hopes of prosperity that wore entertained* year (Igo had been fulfilled ; and if it could not he accurately termed • boom year it had been a year of</p>
        <p>1•00 it ER. IA I. Atil %ITV Aril, NW; RE,. OM, dark end menacing feature of 1910 wee the 'armor dispute. with their, slaw at prewent inevitable reaulta— strikes and lock-mits. Ina neighbouring country there had twee one of such magnitude as for a brief period almost to paralyze the commercial mid social life of the Kingdom. In this countro we liad happily eiicaped such a calamity, hut there bed been disputes 111.1....011 employers and employwl of more then ordinary magni- tude. One ominous feature bed been the unwillingnees of the men to follow the advice of their lenders or to abide by the term, which bad been agreed upon. chat Wan</p>
        <p>ATAI. 1.01.1cr</p>
        <p>end if pinned would lead to the Tom o( trale unioniem. Trade unionism sea of vital neceivrity to the working- men. Not only vise it his support in case of nicks... or lons of employment, but by combination only was be alibi to aefegueril bi• own intereets when raisunder- stawlings arose with his employers. The trade union lenders spolin with authority es ,epreeentiog an Immense body of workmen, hut if their ilecisirms were repudiated by the nine their representative authority ceased and the individual workman or the workmen in one particular faitory would have to fight alone in the beit wriy they could. It was too much to hope that dispotos would not arise, but surely it wne not ton much to hope that $OlOO In, barbarous system of nottlement might be adopted. thin strike. and lock-outs of lest year involved 500,005 worker.; the time lost in these disputers aggregated over, 000,000 working days. it wee the dory of Chambers of Com- merce esrnestly to support any legislation that wee likely to bring about NMI, letter form of eettlement. Something better h.l bee, done alroady,and tostimony should l,n borne to the interest and action taken by the Board of Trade in ohs dispoters of lest year, which Action generally helpo.l to soccossf result. Chambers of Commerce might do something to dispel the fatal idea that</p>
