        <p>, TUE 'timid's,: or Tilr rho' si. I iv of the country was in very much the same ',coition that, it was a year .go. It bad been the subject of much Nay eloquence, but bad not had that calm study and consideration which its Importance deserved. It was to be hoped the time woo coming whet it might cell. •tobe • mere Party cry, but might be discussel by Chamber, of Commerce and other assemblies of thoughtful end onpryjudicel men. • Hear, hear.) With regard to the prospects of the prnent year they might talcs encoureffement from the fact that there we.. decide Ily hopeful feeling, not only in this country Idit abroad. been in the rnited Stet's'', where trade during the laid year had been declioing, there si ere dor indica- tion. of a revival. It was true that nowhere did they reb the sign• a immensely Increased business awl inflated prices, and perhaps It was well that they did not. Such thues of feyeriah activity were invariably followed by extravaganca, fear and depression. With- out undue optimism he thought they might look for a steady and increaaing trade is bleb would bring with it RX s natural consequence improving prices and in:reseed prosperity to both employers and eciployed. (Applause.)</p>
