        <p>CORONATION _ARRANGEMENTS. The Date of Walking Day.</p>
        <p>The I ienersi 13111 T. neol CnIDIIIIIIPP has resolved that the Mayor be requested to convene a Town'. Meeting to consider the congestion of the Education Committee. that Walkunr Day he changed from Friday. the 3,ra, to Friday the 23rd June 011 aeciunt of the Coronation . .</p>
        <p>o. . Resolved. els°. that Ole following MenliVr• he :Ippointed a committee to consider sod rers.rt on the , steps to be token in connection with tLe Comnation 'of the King on the ".!'2.nd Jnne next —The %vol., the Deputy-Mayer, Alderman Bennett, Conocillons Bellian. Itirtici, Dickenre. Jenkins. Pemberton, Roberts, Steel, Toole nod Wilkinson.</p>
        <p>EDUCATION COMMITTEE CRITICISED. An interesting education summons wits heard at the Borough Police Conn, on Tuesday morning, when William Eckel.ley, it master-bnker, of 1...1g100-lane. Warrmgton, was kummoned for not sending his child to school. Defendant stated !het he hal been before the Metal. Committee aud they informed him that they were quite sure that he was hoeing his child edttrate;l in the proper manner. A retration who sum n qualified teecher taught the child regularly at home. Defendant ridded that he thought the committee were carrying thin...to a ridiculous extent. The Chairman (Councillor Henry Roberts): Ye. •xense is that the child is being efficiently taught. 8.. t I don't think von have my right to say that tie, Edncation Committee is carrying things ton ridicule. Went. As Chairnan of the EdUCAtiOn Committee I want lorry that the committee carries nut its onerous snd responsible duties Inn very satisfactory manner. I think you ought to withdraw your reworks. Defendant : Well I will withdraw them. The Chairman t I nrn sure that my rellengura Slink that yen bore not willinely neglected the education of your 'child. Coder the cirri...knees the case will Ile dismissed with the understanding that the child incest to ichot t. . . _</p>
        <p>AroNna E.—Before the Mayor tin the chair), Alderman Forahaw, Alderman James Evens, Councillor .1. Wilkinson. Mel's,. H. Thornton, S. Wright, W. Roberts. G. Pilling. T. Locker. and W. Furness. Meurt Ano Anot"r NnTIIING.—The case, in which Dow,' ft Stockton Ilseith butcher,wa• charged he John Henry Hackney, another butcher of lintitsford• road, Warrington, with anganit, which wag adjourned six weeks a go,carne npagain for hearing.—M r.Steelw be revesented complainant, explained that the ease was heftre the bench sis weeks ago when it was adjourned in order that. witneas then on board a boat ready to sail, end who woeld not he beck for sis weeks. might appear.—Mr. Steel now announced that tie witness had lees delayed by bad weather and could not possibly be beck for another week. For that reason be staked for a further adjournment of a week.—Mr. Brown nointed cot that at the last hearing he had said that if Mr. Steel's client would apologise kin client was quite ready to apologia* elan. Mr. Brown added that a friend of both defendant and complainant, had consented to act as arbitrator between the two tradesmen, during the week's adjournruent.—Thia course Wel. adopted. A REPENTANT PHISONER.—An fCbonf the Arpley fields emelt was beard when John Stok•ttEdeardestreet wag charged with stealing El in cash from a Mr. James Grounde.in November.l9o9.—lt can gusted by the Chief Constable that the prisoner was brought to the police station on Saturday in ronstquenee of a prisoner, who is now serving six montha imprisonment, issiline a certain communication in which be stated that he desired to net everything cleared up in ruder that he might Mart afresh when he left prigon. In hie elate- meet he implicated the priartier in the dock into a theft es far back &amp;sign.. It appeared that kir. Grounds on November Ist. 1903, returned home late at night when he found that his honse had been broken open and £4 in money stolen. At that time he did not report the matter to the police. The prisoner Stokes, wan brought to the police atation on Saturday night. and told that he would be charged with the theft and be straight away admitted it. He would like to ask for an adjournment, the Chief Constable added, as certain formalities would have to he gone through vrilh the Hume Office in order that the other prisnner aught be brought theta—The CaSe ant remanded for 7 days. - •</p>
        <p>TEESDAY.—Refor. Councillor, Henry Roberta (in the chair), C. Jarkson Holm. and Mr. W. Richardson. Atainnirs hictio OF THE Loot-FIRM FRAl'll,—Mr. Hallaway, on behalf of the police, applied for an order that the goods aerptestrated by the police in correction with the Long Firm frond., abould Oc dispoaed of. He reminded the Retch that the case was before the Court a fortnight ago and the pro- ceedings were adjourned in order that further inquiries might be made by the 'Yeirif Constable with regard to the ownetahip of toe gond'e. There war only One claim, added Mr. iinilasir•y, that wee represented by Mr. 3enkine. and he had offered to glee no ondertakittg that would indemnify the police against any further proceedings that might he mode in respect of the goods.—The Chairman : We will make the order. A DOM FSTIC SERVANT.% FR A I.II.—MeHnOR Wilcox, n domestic aeroant, was charged on remand with steal- ing, by false pretences, I articles of 'traceries, the property of a Mr. John William Jones—lt was atated thot the prianner was before the Court a week no, when it wait stated that she went to the complainant and said that ale represented Mrs. Law, the wife of Dr. Law. It appeared, however. that prisoner had hen in Mrs. Law. enipiny hut bed been dismissed. Inquirits had been made in Torquay where prisoner had eon is friends, hut they refused to have anything to do with her.—The prisoner ooneented to go in a home and it wee decided to adopt that course. . .</p>
        <p>A Vns,ss RAD Rrcoun.—A long list of convictions to the tune of 16—all since 191,8—were read out against Agnes Ryan. no settled sttode who was charged on remand with stealing from the person of Albert Boyle, silver watch valued El 17P. Gd —lt were stated by the Chief Constable that the prisoner stopped Boyle and anted him for a match and a cigarette. Sne was supplied with what she eslred for, and then asked if she could light bee cigarette by him. Ile did this and immediately after she had left, hint Be 3 in found that his watch hod pone. lie reported the matter to the police, und riPoner wits arrewed in endeavouring to nunn the watch at the pawnshop of Alr. Tyrer. After thm number of con. intim a against priscner hod been read out the Bench sent her to prison for three months with herd labour.</p>
        <p>WFONE4DAY—Before Moser.. Charles White in the chair), C. Jackson Holmee end W. T. Williams. A HE ARTL DISFRI 100.—The story of bow a man deserted hie flve children, was related when Chntles XlcGuire, 24, West-etreet. was charged with neglecting his children—Mr. A. Browne, who prosecuted na behelf of the N.S.P.C.C. said that prisoner wan ru underhand puddler. his wife died some three month. ego. and hi. children wern taken in by his brother. Ile paid for their keep, surn of 225, n week, hint after a time he reduced the money to 20s. per week. A month before Christmee prisoner wria out of employ- ment and he told bin brother that be was out of work owing to extra work being put on him with no extra pay. Enquiries were made by the brother at the Whitecross cm., where prisoner had heen working, and it was found that primmer , Lad been dismissed owing to his baring neglected hie duties. Enter, however, he got work at Dallara FOT,P. but on January 22nd he dieappeared leaving his children, with the result that they were sent to the Workhouve on the 24th. McGuire, Mr. Browne added, had already deserted his children flve time. before.— The Clerk :What have _you to .on?— Intoner : Nothing, air.—lt wee stared by Inepector Wainwright that on one occasion in 1907, prisoner went sway and left his wife and family, who were turned nut into the etreet et len o'clock et night.—Prisoner .r in] he was very sorry for abet had occurredand the Chsirman sent him to prison for three mouths with hard labour.</p>
        <p>Tti URSDAV.—lterore Mem, A. J. Jolley (in the chair), W. T. Williams J. Upson: end Dr. Nadel,</p>
        <p>MONP.V.—= Short of prone," was the excuse advanced by Sarah Wrench. is, Oldhsm-ytreet, Warrington, when charged with stealing a •est, the property of a neighbonr. It opposers that prisoner was to the house of a Mrs. Winter, Florence-street, and when she left a vest was found to be missing. Sits: pinion fell on the prisoner and the clothes were found pawn, the ticket hying found in Wrench's possession. Chsitnian, in binding her over for 12 months, said the magistrates were extremely sorry to have primate before them</p>
        <p>FIIII,AY.—Before Alderman Forshaw in the chair!, Alderman Hotpoint, Mews. A. J. Jolley, H. Thorn- ton. G. Pilling and 'l'. Locker. A FAMILY S.,l'Aiilll.l....—Alma Allen, 13, Cumberland- street, charged her husband. Thomas Allen, with ?wasn't. —The wife stated that defendant came home late on Saturday nicht end threw her and the baby out of the bonen at 12.45 on Sunder morning, and would not let her come in again.—Mr. Steel who appeared for defendant) : Do yon really want to tend tour humbend to prison ?—Witness : No, sir, I only went protection. —Tte case was eventually adjourned for four weeks, with a view to bringing shout a reconciliation.</p>
        <p>„ AN ALLFPFD AsssULT IN A TitsNicsti.—ltichard Walters, who is separated from his wife Clara %%alters, Bostock•str•et, Warrington, way charged by her with n•stult, which she alleged took place in a tramcar on Sunday evening.—Mr.Steel, who appeared for the wife, stated tbatthe couple had been ,parated for two yearn On Sunday evening then ifs went to visit some friends called Dendy.who resided in Knutsford.rosd,and ',bent too o'clock she left the house in company with Mr and Mrs Donny end other friends to go to the cnr terminus to Sankey.When they arrived at the terminus the wife saw her husband evidently intent on going on the same car es his wife andas a consequence Mr Doody Accom- panied her to protect her. When they got into the car defendant sat opposite them, and he said to his wife, in the pretence of all the occupants of the car, Tell the pm ?pin who you are, yen—, fie then kicked her in the leg. He tots about to kick her Resin when Dendy stopped him. Mrs. Walters had been in the itlirmary for some time neer Christmas suffering from as internal complaint.—Leslie Ponnialon, the conductor on the ear where the alleged assault took place, stated that when they got to Green- street he noticed defendant deliberately kick Mr, Walters on the k ',c.o.—Defendant in defence denied tickle,/ his wife, and alleged that Dendy had his arm round Airs. Walter's wtiht.—Defendant was hound over to keep the peace for six months.—Weltere then charged Deady with ...molting hint in the car.- -The case wae distaissed.—Mr. Hallaway. Deputy Town Clerk, subsequently. on behalf the Tramways Committee, proaecuted for damage done during the altertatiOn.—The caw was, however, adjourned,</p>
