        <head>..t. iwy ••• • ,</head>
        <p>..t. iwy ••• • ,</p>
        <p>The church of St. James', LstchforiLitithe centre of encouraging and fruitful work, quietly but steadily carried on in in parish of some :,000 inhabitants. Its 'clergy, of whom we give a photograph almve, have the co-operation of a loyal and enthusiastic band of loy workers, and all are unselfishly devoting them- selves to the advancement ot the Church and its numerous organizations. The Vicar (Rev. If. B. Firth M A.) haa had charge of the parish for seven yeae. Ile is a native of Leeds and received his early education at the Grammar Sokol in that city. Then he proceeded to Lincoln College, Oxford where he took the degree of B.A. in 1893 and M.A. in 1807. Ile received a course of theological training at Salisbury College. In 1804 Mr. Firth was ordained deacon and in 1805 priest by the Bishop of Chester. His first and only curacy was at the Parish Church, Runcorn, where he laboured for ten years, with a conscientiousness, tact and zeal which endeared him to the parishioner. lie took a epecial interest in the choir. attending the practices and offering every encouragement to the members. Ile had also charge of the boys. brigade for several years and hock part in the annual encampments. 'rho day and Sunday schools claimed a large part of Ida atten- tion and he was the first curate is Runcorn to hold the position of a manager of the day school. In January. 1904. Mr. Firth was appointed vicar of St. James' Latchford, and at the game time a surrogate for tine diocese of Chester. The congregation at Runcorn showed their eateem and Frodwill by presenting the rev, gentleman with a gild hunter watch and chain, inscribed as follows:—" To the Rev. Hellen Boiaeau Firth, M.A., vicar of St. James' Latchford. A token of affectionate esteem tram Runcorn, his first and only curacy, where his work for ten years has endeared Inin to the parishioners. January 2rlfit, 1901." At Latchford Mr. Firth was confronted with work of a somewhat difficult and nphill character. Reapplied himself to it with marked er.ergy and unquestionably hie efforts have told greatly to the benefit of the Church in this populous neighbourhood. Mr. Firth, who is a very definite Churchman, makes a strong point of enlisting the interest and sympathy of the young people, and it is pleasing to notice that a good proportion of those who pass through the Sunday school become regular =enders at the Church. The</p>
      </div>
