        <head>(Vicar).</head>
        <p>(Vicar).</p>
        <p>Band of Hope and Girls' Friendly Society are in a flourishing condition. The work amongst the men an made good headway, and there is a large and enthusiastic Bible Class which is growing in numbers every year. The large body of regular communicants is also another evidence of sound advance on right lines. Although the pariah is not a rich -on, the congregation contribute liberally to the various funds. Shortly after Mr. Firtli'a •ppointnient to the living a emu of .t:Mfi wan raised and expended on the restoration of the church and iniprovements at the schools and mission. An before indicated, Mr Firth is ttrltinaie in the help which he meek es from the lay workers. A large Church Council, elected by the congregation, ia an important factor in the parochial machinery. The Sunday school ia efficiently con- ducted and Mr. Firth also take. a keen personal interest in the religi us instruction in the day echools. The services at St. James' Church are of a nuderate character, an far as ceremonial is concerned, but care is taken that everything shall be done 'decently and in 'pier" and there are reverence and devotion as well as dignity. The mission church of St. Hilda, which serves the Knutsford-road putt of the parish, is a moat useful and appreciated off-shoot of St. datues'a. It is under the special care of the Rev. H. It. Corsbie. A scheme has been net on foot to build a new Si. Hilda's l'horch and :ilready there in a fund of about .S..'.ltet for that purpose. It is hoped to carry out theundertaking in twoinwalments first toerect a mission building at a coat of lOW and later to pro- vide a new church, the total projected expenditure hieing estimated at .1:3,1110.</p>
        <p>, Th-e Vicar has two loyal and bard-working colleagues in the Revn. H. H. Corabie and E. M. Swift. The Rev. 11. H. Corehir,ol.A. llstlield Hall, Durhsmi took the degree of B.A. in 1009, and M.A. in 1909. He was ordained deacon in 1906 and priest in 1007 by the Bishop of Chester. Ile has 'pen curate at James' since 1906, and St. Hilda's rui-inion has made steady nod estisfaetory advance under his guidance. The Rev. E. M. Swift in a B.A. of the floral University of Ireland, obtaining that dearee in 1009. He was rdairted deacon in IIOnS and priest in 1009 by the Itishoo of Cheater. Like Mr. Corshie, lie is serving hia first curacy at St. James', haring worked in the perish from 1900.</p>
        <p>of St. Jame' Church with wood block., Intend of the cold and noisy atone flag. which exieted et present. There were several other things they required, and he wee hopeful of obtaining wont of the rooney. neceasary. (Hem% herrn After the concert dancing wee engaged in. St. Ann's Church Tea Party.</p>
        <p>The annual tea ned corcert in connection with St. A.'s Church, IN lowick-road, took place on Monday evening in the parish schools, Dannett-street. After tea a concert was given. The Vicar presided and was supported by the Rev. F. C. Shirtctitle, curate. and Mr. E. Winter, churchwarden. An excellent programme of music was much enjoyed. During a brief interval the Vicar returned thanks to tall who had assisted either as subscribers, collectors, caterers, and artistes, sud also announced with great regret the coining severance of Mr. and Mrs. E. Winter from the parish owing to their removal from the town. Ile expressed for himself and all connected with the church ,incers regret nubs - ing, but at the same time wished them and theirs "God speed and every happiness in their new home.' Master 11. Shirtcliff gave two pianoforte solos, the Sent piece entitled" Cnited Kingdom Mnrch," opening the programme. Mite Catterall sang " Into Thy Hands." Mr. Whit." Goodbye sweetheart," and "My own, cry guiding star." Mr. F. Randall tendered two violin solos and Miss Len sting " Thom" A quartette party contributed "Simple Simon " apil afterwards " Torn, Tom, the piper's tern." Then ME Hann. sang "I'm,, eyes of grey "and" Land scross the sea." Mr. L. Miller rendersd on the cornet the solo "Look back." AI mita.. sang "My Tr.sere," and" Love's own sweet song." A duet was given by Mr. and Mrs. Hann., entitled " hey. of Heaven," Mr. Moon sang " When the ebh tide ova," " Rocked in the cradle of the dexT" ; Miss White " Deer home songs," and then " Roses ; Mr.Thomaa "British Boys 'and Mis• Welch " Allan Waters." The occompanitts nunto the Mins. White, Blackburne and Mug.% and Messrs. Randall and Houghton.</p>
