      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-04_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>All Saints Mission.</head>
        <p>All Saints Mission.</p>
        <p>The Importance of Sunday School Work.</p>
        <p>In " St. Paul's Parish Mr mizine " attention is called to the work of the Sunday School attached to All Saints' Mission. The work of the Sunday School (says the writer) is becoming more and more important every year. It is not often that the" 'limes" takes any notice of such matters as Sunday Schools, but hint week they hnd a leading article on the insportence of , Sunday School work. The "Time. " Religious teaching is losing or has lost very much of its prominence in our Elementary Schools, and it is high time that people should do their very beet for the Sun- day Schools. Chnrchpoople most he prepwed to spend both money and time on their Sonday Schools." We thoroughly neree with this seutinieist expressed in our leading English newspaper. Th. Bishop of London elso in his New Year letter to his Dioc.se, asks for help in undertaking a greet and an important work, namely, the prsctical recreation on an alequate and proper hissia of the Sunday school work of the Diocese. Many stich instences may he cited to show that Sunday reform in beginning to take place all over the country ; lint in no parish, perhaps, is it more necessary than in this. The conditions tinder which the Sunday school work is carried on in the %anion district. are simply dreadful. 'fbe accommoda- tion pro .dad in not renlly sufficient for hall the scholars 'h. attend. Colens this state of things is to remain rcendnl to the parish, every effort should be made commence Church extension in the district as soon • possible. %Vs, wish to melte a general appeal to all th people in the Mission district to support the very at of their ability, both the Sundry nchool and its them in their splendid istrorta. it is diillcult to ' esti ate the value of the work of the •teachern, and th shire it is the dirty of the parents to help and s rt them in their work. est deal of the ineanspirliedness of the present e result of tail training in childhood and iv very manifest, in many cones, that the has been trained from childhood in the ood in the vreyv of paltriness end selfish- he parents of our children will see thet on are encouraged enmetirum to give d, and then they will be finite sure nes, wio are so treined, will grow up id and large-hearted men and women. Missionary Festival. issionary Festival which has been e team at Liverpool, lilean and held this year, by the wish of the lance with tie desire of the Runl- et Warrington. The date is now y. the 6th of July. There will elebratinna of the Holy Communion in the town n special service at Perish Church ; e Conference in the afternoon at the Cooseryntive Club ; g of young people at the Parr Hall urs and a public meeting laths same seeing. The Bishop of the Diocese the Conference rind at the public Meet- •re being invited fnr the occlusion e Werriugton Parish Church Parochial e trust will prove one of great benefit og to the town and neighbourhood. His • Mayor It tv most kindly granted the use of all for • luncheon which will be provided</p>
        <p>1911.</p>
        <p>for representatiten from every parish. Further partieu• lars will Le annonoced lets, on.</p>
        <p>St. Merl Church. Annual Tea Party and Concert. The annual tea party in connection with St. Peter's Church is looked forward to. With great pleasure by the members of the congregation and it was not surprising to Had a largo attendance in the schoolroom, on , Monday evening, when the annuli gathering took ' place. Tea was •,1,041 at 6.30 and all the table* in the schoolroom were 11Ited,the several tables being presided orer by Mesdames, Percival, ffteel, Itartacrth, Cowdell, Yeomans, Gronrele, Padmore and Webster. After ten the entertainment took pleas, otter which Councillor Steel presided, and he was eug,.orted on the platform by the in. It. Ferrite] (•ioar the 1t... .1. 11. Garnett (curate), Mr. I'. H. Westistaw and Mr. Thorns. Reddish. During a break In the programme the chairman, in &amp; humorous speech, extended a hearty welcome to all present, and hoped they might nil be spared to meet at such gatherings for many years to come. The work of the church,he Said, won progressingwnd he tr,,etod they would render all the assistance they could to the clergy in carrying on their good work in it difficult parish. The Hen. B. Pritrt cal, thanked all who had given trays or in any manner had awlitted to make the gather- ing • likleocees. Ile reminded them that the Bishop of the diocese would •isit the church during the next month when the ennivereary of the conaecnstion would he celebrated. Ifs eppealed to the pariatd,nere to attend church in Intgenurnbers,erpecially at the Sunday nn:rning service.</p>
        <p>„ „.. Mr. A. E. Pildmore, the organist of the church, had arranged a capital programme, which wan thoroughly enjoyed. Miss Clarke, of Sankey, delighted the midi- puce with her song "Corn, hack to me.” and she also took part in two duets, ono with Mita Taylor in You 1-han't play in eon yard," and the other with Nlr. ['minions Reuben and Rachel." Messrs. lb and A. I Wit hinshaw mere mend to advantage in two duets the moon hath rained her lump above " and Flow, gentle Dena." Ml,llllll. Seam, and Jack Grenvey gave I a oianotorte duet. and Mr. Wit hinslinw gang Mary." Mr. Alfred G monde provided the homeroom part of the programme and delighted the audience, beim, renewal times encored. The second mint of the progrernme con- sisted of n sketch entitlyel- ode John from Vorkehint” by several young people from l'adgate, and it wan thoroughly enjoyed. Mr. Fillmore acted as RC(7,111. pnnist with his onual slit in. Tos proceedings closed Ith the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
        <p>Workingmen's Mission, Bank Quay. 11r. A. H. Crostield at the Congregational Gathering. ; TheWorkingmen's Mission is doingnn eminently useful w••rk on brood lines in a workingclaas district and its efforts ere happily attended with is most encourneing measure Of OnleCenn.At tie annual congregational gather- ing on Monday evening there was a large ettendanceend a review of the year's progress nhowed that every depart- ment of the mi.ion'a operations ix in a flourishing state. The Crosfleld family has always taken a genet°. practical interest in the mission and on Monday the workers were favoured with the presence of Mr. A. H. Croalleld. who was accorded a very hearty reception. About 180 mat down to tea and the entertainment which followed took the form of n recital by Mr. liarniah Ilarnadale, the well-known elocutionist. The items ranged front grove to guy and were all given with Me drematic eff-ct, one of the moat touching being "A sermon that was not preached"-0 recital of a great mining disteder, teaching ill. 441000 that love of God found ita highest expression in love of one'a fellowmen. The humor°tin piecet included "The student,"" The Irish walking match," and " A waiter for One night only," each of which kept the nudience in a state of merriment. During an interval Mr. A. Tilling (secretary of the mission) prettented big annual report which stated that the church membership bad advanced front 98, at the beginning of last year,to 127 at the close. 'rho vitt! number of communicants for the year a. 722 against 528 for the previous year, so incr.. of 224. The overage ntteudance at, the monthly Communion service. was ii 2, a. compsred with 414 in 1909. 'rho ordinary Suntley evening aervices, in the chapel and school, were very well attended, both buildingx having been inconveniently overcrowded on many occasion.. They had recently ettirted an extension fond, sad the s,uooct at present itt the hank was VA 2s. lid. Mrs. Crostield, of Colwyn Bey, had (at considernble c;m1) purchased a piece of land adjoining the chapel, with a view- to admitting ef 010.1.0ni0/1, to the premise., and he was sere he was voicing the feelings of all their members and workers s hen be arid they were more grateful than words could exp.ra for that further atriking token of the continued helpful end kindly interest that Mrs. Cronfield took in the mission and its work. (Applause.) The chapel accounts showed an inconte of 12117 101. fhtd. and hal.ne in hand at the clone of the year of CC Sa. 10)d. There were separate accountn for the Sunday School, Band of hope, I.nagite of Help, Society Clegg, Missionary Fond, Children's lantetn Servi. Fund. Christian Endeavour Society Poor Fond, or. Mrs) Crostield had again very kindly paid the cost of such items an fuel, lighting he. They had again to place on record their high appreciation of the aolendid services Sc, fnithfully rendered by the friends who omupied the pulpit Sunday by Sunday throughout the year. As laid loon he the founder of the mission (the late Mr. John Crosfleld., the undenominational character of the mission had been maintained. and there was remain ly no meson why that shottld not continue, for he vent tired to say there we. no denmninntional church or chapel in Wnrrington which not a larger Suurlsy evening congre- gro ion, comperatively speaking. than theirs. The following had been appnieted ofkcers end committee for the present year —President, Mr. N. /tough ; vice- president, Mr. G. Dale ; trensurer, Mr. T. rnylor ; communion secretary. Mr. It..lones ; ration! Mr. A. 'filling • committee, Mesars. I'. Dale, D. Hill, F. Atkinson. W. 'Worrell. It. Tanner. 'l'. Erlntundn, J. Ilas.ll, A. Glenee, 11. Edward., P. Cleave, S. Milling- ton, T. Woolley, O. Head. They looked beck upon the pestCe, r (the 4(50 since the rnisninin was founded) with heartfelt thank (nine. to God for His gnodnoss, and they looked forward, full of hope and confidence to the future, believing that the Wane spirit of