        <p>Mr. A.ll. Ciro/hewer paid it gave hint great pleasure once more to congratulate Mr. Tillinc, the energetic secreterv, upon is report which, like those which had preceded it, was no extremely able and not the lent vatisfactory report they had had the privilege of hearing from him, both with regard to the excellent work of the chnpel and its organisationn and the wonderful increase in the attendance. at the services. He (Mr. Croalleld) could not but feel that the Bank (Pray mission and school were just shout an good • monument to the memory of his father—repplausei—ea he himself crane member of the family maid wish to see erected. Thetas Mr. John Crosfleld would bare hoen the lost to wish to refer to the minion In any rain glorious spirit. Whet pinned him during his lite-time and what would here given him maisfection had he been alive to-day, wes the progress which had been described in tint admirable report, and the knowledge of the really good work which the chapel end mirsion best enabled FO many devoted men and women to carry not in that pert of the them. He (xIT. Canfield) wets not speaking from hearsay or giving them a suggestion of his own on that point, because he bad often heard his late father say that nothing he ever did in his life had given him an equal Fannon, of satisfaction to tho mission at Bank Quay. They had had a little practical evidence that the interest he took in the miasion and the pride end delight it wan to him to see ite proem., were almred sad very deeply felt in these days by Mrs. Crosfleld— (epplause)—with whom he the speaker/ many a time talked matters over, Ife ern sure everybody in that room felt truly thenkfol that the good work continued and prospered and, above all, they were deeply indebted to the noble-hearted men and women who pave their services week by week and tear by year in support of a cause which built up line end made better homes for the people of that neighboorhood. (Appinuse.) Ile had been glad to hear of the purchase of land that Mrs. Croafield had made, to listen to the figures which showed such a apleedid advance and. not least, to remember the kind of npirit that pervaded that—he could not say denomination, because it wee not a denomination. (Hear, hear,. That was ex ictiv whet hin father lawns used to say—that he wee eo thankful that at Rank Quay they had only got the broad sect Chriatiane—(hear, hniq—.o that ell those narrow little diversities of creed and the unfortunate spirit of rivalry were ithsent from that mission. fhey could ell meet there united by a broad faith in the enpel of humanity. (Appleuse.) They hail hail a most delight- ful entectainment that evening and in one of the excellent pieces that had hem, recited with such ariiiity there wan a reference to the love of God which was no well expressed in the love of tun. That, stnr ell, was the truth at the root of the teaching at that minion end it was that spirit whin', could unite men of .11 creeds and, he was almost going to say, of none. it was a fine thing ton that in addi• tioo to the spiritual instruction there wee a useful social. benevolent and practical work. They could well feel prond that, it win in that pert of Warrington that the Guild of Help Ind its origin. .Applause.i Baring exprened on behalf of his wife, regret that she could not sauced that internting Tethering, Mr. rroslielil sail tint to come down to Bank Quay *as to remind oneself of very early filly., benne almost his earliest recollections were associated with building castles in the white sand at Bank Quay—he was efraid more than forty years ago. (Laughter and applauee). His most not say nything further einia ramie rlornotr, or that excellent paper the a Esenhiner " would he poking fun at bins. (Laughter,. lie thanked them for their more than kind welcome and atilt more did he rejoice that they could meet nay Iv year to celebrate See !metesa of one of the most nits tide Brained°aa they bed in old Werrington. ( Applause).</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Dnt.E propoyed a vote of thanks to Mr. A. Croeffeld and referred to the groat help which members of the Crosflrild family had extended to the mission from its inception. The raiesion workers were trying to break down the herriert which coparreed different religiose curomonities and prevented the exerciee of that brotherly love and mutual respect that ought to be shown.</p>
        <p>The vote Ness enthusiastically passed and Mr. CRW,FIELD briefly responded.</p>
