      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-04_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A.</p>
        <p>There was a good attendance on Sunday, presided over by the secretery, who nave the r114.116er, two or three leading thoughts in an addreas delivered by the It.. Fred Ilibbert, on Saturday at Ilyecroft Congrega• Tional Church, Ashton-under-Lyne, at the annual meeting of the Lauctwhire and neighbouring counties P.S.A. Federation. From incidents in the Old Testament he drew the !mottos that whatever power we had, however little it mixhn trope., was acceptable in God's service. and that we must be preptred to face knowing that Cool would give us the necessary strength. Mr. II im•worth gave the address open ''Persistent Memories." Ile said revert denial of his Plaster haunted him and iln could not f irget it. There were in our own experiences ninny thing. we would prefer to forpet, but which we could not. The nail was drawn bat the hole WII• left. Evil memories haunted us, and punished us, hut let an cultivate thin. good and pleats/Int. Then we would have happy memories. Mr. Joseph Owen and Mr. A. Healey triou took part in the meeting. Fir. Peyton prceiderl at t. organ. A vote of eyratmthy wn• penned to Mr. and Min. Hibbert nn.".. family end Mr. W. Harem..., on the death of Met. Davenport. Mr. Ilibhert had been a good friend to the P.S.A. in years gone by, with his practical help and sympathy. Th. Chairman said they could not forget him and his in this hour of trouble.</p>
      </div>
