        <p>Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church</p>
        <p>During the course of s talk to the children attending the Ilewsev-road Weeleyan Church, on Sunday morning,. the lie,. E. B. Keeling skid that if Christians in this country were only r• half in earnest as those who had been converted in foreign lands, there would ha it vast difference between the size of the congregations now assembling in churches and chapels. In his aermon Mr. Keeling took for his teen " And they understood not that saying and were afraid to ask Him." lark 320.) There was nothing uncommon in that, for they read of people being taken with a great fesr—they were astonished at the majesty of God, an it said in one place While there mess greet deal that was familiar ;• and friendly, find while there was no barrier tetween God and Ilia loyal children, and even between Him and the sinner, yet every tow and then they came across the PPOSO Of tear, the sense of see. Tilers was one kind of worship that mu already too solemn, too tre-nendons, and there was another kind that was too familiar, Merest impertinent. And, re usual, they had to avoid the evils of both. There was to victim withoitt Its shadow end nearly every vice was exaggerated •irtue. At the point in the Bible which they were discussing they came in touch with the mystery of the Cross and somehow wherever the disciplescame in contact with that they got the sense of veiled fear and anxiety and regret. When JIOIOII told His disciples that Ile mu going lobe crucified, end after three daya would rise again they were afraid to ask Him what he meant. What were they afraid of? Generally sneriking the fear of asking questions in the matter of Cnriatinn truth, especially where the Cross was concerned, wea the fear of having them snew-ered. Many of those vagne, queer, uneasy dotibt• never came ant—they did riot get them in the society clam or Sunday school because they did not want them settled. They rather liked and enioyed the doubts so they did not ask that they should lie simmered. The disciples seemed to be afraid to ask questiona where the Co., was concerned and be !the preacher) was not twits sure that the leer had altogeth• Pr died sway. In 119 cases out of it WO fear mall wrong. The cowardice of ignorance was one of the saddest thing. in the world ttpday. He could not help thinking that the disciples mimed s great chnnce. Becatme they did not ask (111 PA. inns, certain things regarding the explanation ot the atonement were permanently lost to the world. They should not be afraid of searching for truth in regard to the eternal questions, and many of their marchings would be 60iVeO by prayer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-04_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
