        <p>St. John's Presbyterian _Church</p>
        <p>On Son lay evening last Sr 7 Jr.ho's Church W. filled elniost to its fullest capacity. The Ilea. W. Reid chooeing for his test the words of li,mans i., IC, "For I am not ashamed of the Gospel of Christ, fort is the power of God unto salvation to every one that believeth," paeached a powerful and impresaire sermon, which was listened to with the °lomat attention throughout. The tent, said NH. Reid, made known aud declared to them that the condition of mankind sees such that it called for some power unto salvation. Mankind was in thatcondition that a power was reloired that nould MVP. The very •liIItAMCB of heathen religions twilled to the name thing. The great cry of man's soul was for salvation, and that cry came from •Il races of the world. In St. Paul's Inc. just as now, the world awns s d and complaining, seeking for thin power that would save. Paul Was conuious that he had it. and that it was the power I of God—God Himself giving forth His power through this channel. The first element of poorer in the Gospel of Jesus Christ was the gospel teaching of sin, and Ithat teaching WM that sin was a very real tbing,• disease striking its roots deep in the human soot. That way the power that was dragging manisind downwards, end anti! there wets power greater than it. they would I never rise. Bat, tars the apostle, I believe the gospel :to be the power of God. From the gospel teaching of sin they came to the gospel teaching of atonement lbat didn't show God as au angry judge to any amulet, Let them consider the psrabla of the prodigal man,</p>
