      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-04_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>ALL-BRITISH SHOPPING WEEK.</head>
        <p>ALL-BRITISH SHOPPING WEEK.</p>
        <p>DISCUSSION BY WARRINGTON ,CHAMBER OF COMMERCE</p>
        <p>"A Two-edged Weapon."</p>
        <p>An interesting discussion took place at the annual meeting of the Warrington Chamber of Commerce on Monday on a proper' to organize an all-British shopping week for Warrington. Mr. H. HINODON introduced the question, pointing out that the proposal had made considerable progress in London and they might well consider the advise- r extending it to Warrington. Although it wan a matter that chiefly concerned the reirkil trade, he believed there was a retail ass, notion in AVar- rington and it would probably be advisable for the Chamber to send a recommendation to that association, eaying how much they sympathised with the object in view and that if they could do anything in their individual capacity to help, they would be glad to do no. Personally he ;thought that, seeing they were going to have an all-British shopping week in London, at would be a greet advantage if tho provinces and particularly the large industrial towns could co-operate and have one on the same data. If some of their lady friends could be induced to take a little more interest in the country of origin of the articles they bought, it would indirectly beneficially affect the prosperity of this country.</p>
        <p>Ttie Sk:I:IIETARY read a'letter from the Union Jack Industries League bringing the all-lb iti,h shopping movement under the notice of the Chamber.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. R. YOUNG asked whether the object was to give prominence to British goods, irrespective of quality. Mr. KlNGoott replied in the affirmative. They wished to have in all the shops a display of British goods, whether good, had or Indifferent. Mr. Youso said it appeared to him to be a piece of bunkum. . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Xi nor said that, speaking broadly and apart from the fiscal question, there sae no doubt that if more people were anxious to buy British stuff they could get it. The Loper of one of the bignest drapery houses in London said he had to follow popular opinion and that if he had £l,OOO to spend on goode for his establishment he would spend £3OO in England and 11700 abroad. When they saw a statement like that it made them think. Ilis (the speaker's) great bei f• our wee the num who bought the foreign motor car. Ile did not hesitate to say that was • disgraceful thing and the roan who did it wee usually a Conservative and Protectionist. (Litaghter). Mr. YOUNG : And be goes running about advising people to shut out foreign goods. Mr. C. E. PARKER : Would it not be appropriate for . . . . .</p>
        <p>members of the Chamber to ear that for three months</p>
        <p>they won't buy foreign material ? Mr. KINGIION : You cannot do that. You would stop your business. Mr. PARKER That is the point. We are asking others to do what we ourselves would not do. The CHAIRMAN (Mr. Lofthouse) No, we are not asking them to do that. We are merely asking them to ornament their windows with British and half-mode British goods. We are not pledging them not to buy any others. Mr. KINUDON : The object is really to encourage the spirit in the purchaser o' being interested in the origin of the article le buys. Continuing, he said it must be a fact patent to everybody that in the case of the ordinary man or woman who was not directly associated with production, the idea never entered his or her head to inquire where an article was made. Mr. P Malt : Very often the shopkeeper himself does not know. Mr. KINI.I.oN said that in Frml•ham it was impossible to get an English HHI,IO g 3,14 mantle. Nothing but German-made mantles were mocked, although better mantles were manufactured at Stockport. • Mr. It. T. FAIRCI.OUGH said that from an educational point of vieum it would be a very good thing to have an all-British week. There were plenty of ladies who cover thought of where the article' they purchased</p>
        <p>were made. If they had an all-British week they , would know what was made in this country and what was mado abroad. Mr. F. A. Fioerr. It would be disastrous if the scheme al.ould lead to an all anti-British week on the Continent and in other coon tries. It is rather a two- edged weapon. If we offend the foreigner we offend our best customer.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN asked, supposing during the week a customer inquired loran article which wan foreign mode, whether the shopkeeper was under a pledge not to supply it? Mr. KINI:DIIN Absolutely no. It doesn't make the slightest ditieience to the shopkeeper's business.</p>
        <p>The SECRETARY (Alderman: Bennett) maid the idea WAR simply to show what English manufacturers could produce. If they could produce g. ods with equal advantage it wan the right thing to support our own industries. He did not see that it had any con- nection with the fiscal question at all. Mr. FRONT If these things get known abroad, they may ause ill-feeling with regard to England, and we ;lon' t wain any ill-feeling among our beat cuatomers.</p>
        <p>Mr. Tom: said he was given to understand that whereas ladies in Enghind bought an enormous , quantity of Continental silks and ilihons,the tailoring shops in Continental centres bought Itritish cloth. Would they like retaliation from the foreigner? He thought the idea of an all-British week wee a little I bit. petty-fogging. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-04_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>M. P 11097: i t coo supp/y you</head>
        <p>M. P 11097: i t coo supp/y you</p>
        <p>-You patronise your own country when</p>
        <p>The SECRETARY said the matter was one which more directly concerned the Tradesmen's Association. If the Chamber approved of it, there was no reason why it should not give the scheme its bleseing. Mr. K I.ON proposed, and Mr. Fritzrt.ormj seconded, that the matter be mentioned to the Trades- men's Association with the general intimation that the Chamber svmpathi4ed with the proposal which it would like to see carried out in Warrington. Mr. PARK ER : It is a matter that concerns them</p>
        <p>more than it concerns us. I feel we are recommend- ing them to don thing that wean Imaiiteas men would not do. ,</p>
        <p>Mr. kisnoos We have not any retail distribution. It a man has a retail shop, all we ask Mtn to do is to separate the Britian and non-British goods—to put the British sluff in the window f..ir a week and keep the non-British stuff in the cellar. The resolution was carried, several iitembere refraining from voting.</p>
      </div>
