      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-04_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>WARRINGTON CHAPTERS. THE CITY OF THELWALL</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CHAPTERS. THE CITY OF THELWALL</p>
        <p>A Familiar Inscription and the History Behind it.</p>
        <p>An inscription which many of our readers must have puzzled over is written large upon the gable of the "Pickering Arms " at Thelwall.</p>
        <p>IN THE YEAR 920 KING EDWARD THE ELDER FOUNDED A CYTY HERE AND</p>
        <p>CALLED IT THELWALL.</p>
        <p>Emblazoned thus in the centre of the tiniest of villages there is a boldness in the statement that arrests the most careless traveller, and enquiries leap quickly to the mind.</p>
        <p>Who was King Edward the Elder? And is it true that a thousand years ago a city had risen here at his call? If eo, how came this now-forsaken bit af country to be chosen as its site?</p>
        <p>If two corrections are made, the statement, may he taken as correct. Firstly it was in the year of the Christian era 923 that Edward spent an Autumn and Winter here ; and secondly, it was not a city, but a burgh or fortified circle in which he dwelt. The stirring events that brought him to Thelwall deserve careful study for they had great consequences in our rough island story; but the bridging of so great a span of time</p>
        <p>commanded the burgh to be built on the south side of the river, opposite to the other; and the bridge over the Trent, betwixt the two burghs: and then went into Peakland, to Bakewell, and commanded a burgh to he built and manned there in the immediate neighbourhood.</p>
        <p>These quotations are mere fragments from a long record and are chosen because of the mention of Runcorn, Thel wall, and the Con- quest of Manchester. It must now be shown how these places came to be of importance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-04_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>THELWALL A THOUSAND YEARS AGO.</head>
        <p>THELWALL A THOUSAND YEARS AGO.</p>
        <p>THE PICKERING</p>
        <p>SHOWING TIIE F</p>
        <p>is a large mental exercise and takes us to conditions strangely remote.</p>
        <p>ENGLAND IN SAXON DAYS. A thousand years ago the Mersey was an important dividing line. The centre of England was called Mercia, and had passed under the rule of the Saxon kings from the South. The Anglo-Saxons had then blended to form the English race, and along with the British in Wales had been influenced by Christian teaching for two centuries or more, and indeed were nominally Christian.</p>
        <p>Alfred the Great had Used his fine genius to make a nation, but during his reign had been greatly distressed by the Danes. These heathen pirates had crossed the sea and overrun the North of England, , carrying destruction wherever they had gone, and Alfred had died leaving them checked and weakened, but not subdued.</p>
        <p>The struggle was not only a racial one. It had an aspect far more important, for it seemed to Alfred that if the Danes prevailed, Christianity would be destroyed and a fierce paganism take its place. This aspect of the struggle nerved his son and daughter, and I both Edward and Athelfla3d grew up with a courage that was worthy of their cause. Alfred the Great died in 901, leaving the immense task of holding the Danes and building up the kingdom to his son and daughter.</p>
        <p>Though no careful description has come down to us we have knowledge which helps us to form a fairly clear picture of the sur- roundings of Thelwall. The fruitful soil of Cheshire was then forest land save where it had been cleared by the husbandman. Groups of wattle huts used by the charcoal. burners .ør small farmers formed the only villagers and here and there a tiny church where, in a language of the then Litany, they prayed,</p>
        <p>From Danes end all other savage men Flom Wolves and nil other savage besets (loot! Lord deliver as.</p>
        <p>The Mersey spread itself over the flat country through which it flowed, and had made ugly marshes. At Thelwall the ground rose a little and would stand dry and strong above the soft marsh. Its position would probably allow it to be easily defended and Edward pitched upon it as he had done upon IRumorn and made it an outpost of the I kingdom of Mercia.</p>
        <p>Looking north from.his foitified ".burgh</p>
        <p>the King would view a hostile territory, for the Danes had spread themkelves across the county from Northumbria to the Irish sea and beyond lay the British Kingdom that extended from the Clyde to the Ribble.</p>
        <p>ARMS, THELWALL</p>
        <p>iiI.IOUS INSCRIPTION</p>
        <p>Manchester from its sheltered place hetweet the river branches had commended itself as a place of defence and the Danes were here entrenched. From his fortress at Thelwall Edward marched down upon them and succeeded in driving them but.</p>
        <p>The autumn and winter of A.D. 923 were spent between the fortified circle at Thelwall and the newly acquired outpost of Manchester, and then with the spring of 924 Edward passed to Nottingham and Peakland to build fresh defences againt his foes.</p>
