      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-04_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>EDWARD THE ELDER.</head>
        <p>EDWARD THE ELDER.</p>
        <p>The title "Elder " has been given to Edward as a mark of distinction. He was in reality the first Edward to fill the English throne. After him there came two other Edwards and these have received the dis- tinguishing titles the Martyr and the Confessor. If we counted our rulers from Alfred (as certainly we ought to do) instead of from William the Conqueror these would be known as Edward I. 11. and 111., and our late king would have been Edward the tenth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-04_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>ANGLO-SAXON CHRONICLE.</head>
        <p>THE ANGLO-SAXON CHRONICLE.</p>
        <p>The document which gives us our chief light on the events of a Millenium ago is called the "Anglo-Saxon Chronicle." To describe it would be a large and engaging task, but for the present purpose it will suffin.e if we quote a few passages that reveal the movements of Edward of Ethelfreda in our immediate neighbourhood.</p>
        <p>In this year, God. granting, Ethelffrd , lady of the Mercians, went with all the Mariana to Tamworth, and built a burgh there, in the elirly summer; and before the following Lammas (Auguat lot) that at Stafford. 'thou in the year after this, that at Eddiabury, in the early summer; and afterwards, in the same year, towards the end of autumn, that at Warwick. Then in the next year, after Mid-winter, titat at Clhirbury and that at Wordbury ; and that same year, before Midwinter that at Runcorn.</p>
        <p>In this year King Ladweard west, after autumn, with a force to Thelwall, and com- manded the burgh to be Inuit, and inhabited, and manned ; and commanded another force also of the Mercian nation, while he there sat, to reduce Manchester in Northumblia, and repair and man it.</p>
        <p>In this year, before Afidsommer, Kiog Eadweard went with a force to Nottingham, and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-04_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>EDWARD'S TRIUMPH.</head>
        <p>EDWARD'S TRIUMPH.</p>
        <p>His strenuous days met their reward. In he words of the " Anglo Saxon Chronicle": And the Kings of the the North Welsh, Howell, and Cledauc, and Scotland, and all the North Welsh race, sought him for lord. And all the people who were settled in the Ifericans' land . . . . .</p>
        <p>submitted to him, with Danish and English.</p>
        <p>The sympathetic writer of the Chronicle becomes eloquent as be narrates the final</p>
        <p>triumph: And then the king Of the Scots and all the nation of the Scots, and all those who dwell in Northninbi ia, as well Englieh an Danish and Nortlimen and others, and also the king of the Strathclyde Welsh, chose him for father and for lord.</p>
        <p>But the strenuous fighter did not long enjoy his triumph. He died at Farndon in 924, and was laid to rest at Winchester. His brave sister JEthelflied died in the midst of her connicts two years earlier, and was carried from Tamworth to be buried in the Church of St. Peter, Qloucester. The struggle in which Thelwall played a part was an important one. Though apparently a conflict of race it was really a conflict of ideals and religion, and had Alfred and Edward been defeated there seems little doubt that the progress of England would have been delayed for centuries. In the end the Danes did triumph, but Christian teach- . .</p>
        <p>ing had done its Work ere this, and they mingled with the English to form a common race. EDWARD THE ELDER AND EDWARD VII.</p>
        <p>It is a coincidence that just a thousand Xxi t 4 years separated the coronation of he first Edward and the last. Edward t Elder was crowned at Kingston-on-Tha es on Whit Sunday, 902, and the stone.on w 'eh he sat may still be seen in the village reet. The tenth Edward was crowned in 19 And if we may place a contrast of the coincidence, the tenth Ed his side Alexandra, a daughter cl whom the first Edward spent holding back</p>
        <p>THE PICKERING ARMS INSC Much more might be writte latest about the stirring days c the Danes, but we have onl its local aspect.</p>
        <p>Those who have prepared Arms" inscription have m the error from the Sevent</p>
        <p>antiquary, Peter Leycester, " In the year of Christ 920 surnamed the Elder, built Thelwall, though now an obs( called Tests Florilego, from stumps rut from the trees, had environed it about as Saxons,called such stakes Thel Wall is a word yet used for a encompasseth any place abou other thing or building. t Brittiania, Cheshire and tha made it a garrison and I{ therein wherewith also agt</p>
        <p>Wigorinerusis. But it seems to lie waste in the time of the Conqueror for I find no mention of it in the Domesday Book:'</p>
        <p>Leycester seems to have been unacquainted with the " Anglo Saxon Chronicle "and is quoting from other ancient historians one of whom may have wrongly copied it from thence.</p>
        <p>al - ide at es</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-04_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>MERSEY FISHING RIGHTS.</head>
        <p>MERSEY FISHING RIGHTS.</p>
        <p>Leycester has a mention of Thelwall also in reference to fishing rights in the river. "Roger of Pqicton was lord of all the land in Lancashire, between the rivers of Ribble and Mersey; and gave half of the fishing of Thelwall to the Abbot of Shrewsbury, under Henry the first. This was the fishing on the Lancashire side. " The prior ef Norton had the other half of the fishing of Thelwall, on the Cheshire side, granted by William constable of Cheshire the younger, baron of Halton, about the reign of king Stephen. . .</p>
        <p>"In our mize book Of Cheshire, anciently the abbot of Salop stood charged with three shillings in the mize for fishing in Thelwall ; but this mize is totally lost, no man now living being able to inform us who ought to pay the same.</p>
        <p>" I conceive, after the dissolution of Abbeys in England by Henry the Eighth, the fishing coming into the kings hands, the mice , thereof ceased being not at all paid of late times, the fishing being then of little value.; If nobody bothered aoat Mersey fishing in the days of Henry VIII, the subject is hardly likely to be opened in the reign of George V. A.M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-04_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>1,-,,,,,,,-...-,,,,,,, CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB. •,--PV-...e....-:,••••••••••,01,%%43</head>
        <p>1,-,,,,,,,-...-,,,,,,, CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB. •,--PV-...e....-:,••••••••••,01,%%43</p>
        <p>THE BARN OWL TELLS A STORY. No, I am not a chicken-stealing owl, though I think the by I am going to tell you about must have thought so. .</p>
        <p>- I am a barn owl, and so far as chickens are concerned, I never meddle with them. In fact, if a good fat pullet should come to me and ask to be eaten, I should bow my thanks and send her away. The fact that I am a barn owl and not a hooting chicken stealer, ought to be known to every farmer's son, but some of them are too stupid to learn natural history.</p>
        <p>Such a one -came into the barn where I was stopping the other day. I had entered the place the night before and caught four , fat mice and a big rat. After devouring them, I flew up to roost on a big beam and went to sleep. When night came again, I was to go for more rats and mice, and if left alone for a week or so. I could clean the barn of vermin and make the farmer rejoice. I was sound asleep and having a pleasant dream, when the barn doors banged open and a boy about fifteen years old banged in. He went kicking things about, and whistling as he kicked, and by and by he happened to look up and se me. " Whew ! but there's an owl! "he shouted, at the top of his voice. - " Well, what of it'?" I asked, as I looked down at him.</p>
        <p>"But I've got to have your life!" " Why ?" "Because you are a bird—because you are an owl."</p>
        <p>'But lam a barn owl, and live on rats and mice." "That makes no difference,' he said, and went hunting for stones to throw_ at me. Ho did throw at me a couple of times and then, as he was stooping over for the third time, I flaw for him and alighted on his head. I gave him a couple of sharp digs with my Claws, and then fastened them into his cap and flew out of the door and away to another barn. I heard him shouting and calling, but I did not look back. I have his cap yet, and if could I write as well as some of the boys and girls that read this page. I should put the following advertisement in the papers: "If the stupid boy that didn't know the difference between a barn owl and a chicken stealer, will read up on natural history and beg my pardon besides, his cap will be left at his father'. kitchen door the first dark night after this." TIIE TWINKLY STAR.</p>
        <p>Although I'm sorry when it's time to go to bed at night, I never feel a bit atraid when Nerve puts out the light: For even if it seemv an cleric as ever it van Ice,</p>
        <p>I'm Sure to have a little mar to keep no company.</p>
        <p>I pnll the curtain back, and asl lie there in ifly bed, can ore it twinkle, twinkle in the sky above my</p>
        <p>And p'earrs you won't believe it, hut I'm just au sure</p>
        <p>That wijen-it-st•es me kiss my hand it twinkles all the more.</p>
        <p>Although I s'pose it's too for off to hear rne when I at milt, We often have a little game at hide and meek : I hide beneath the bedclothes end it hides behind a cloud, And when we both peep out again it twinkles very loud.</p>
        <p>One night I. saw a star fall out of bed—oh such a</p>
        <p>It ally have been by accident, it may have been in play— Rut anyhow, I felt as glad as ever I could be, That it waa not the twinkly one that always laughs at me!</p>
        <p>Ada Leonora Harnis.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-04_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>WONDERFUL CURE OF PSORIASIS</head>
