        <head>Dr. Williams' Pink Pills.</head>
        <p>Dr. Williams' Pink Pills.</p>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>Harry Tate's Companies can invariably draw good audiences wherever they go, for they always remind the public a the great comedian himself. As a rule the farces underi,,,,n by his companies are comedies which stand prtrioniinan, aimugst the lesser lights that gather round th.zni. So the absurdity " Flying," which top, the LW at the Palace this week is full of -r.tekling with humour, and rho, wiling tid, Int 11,019 situations. As music-hall cc. In in every conceivable thinc,- it sur• ;sing that aeroplanes and the art of 0.,,ag generally should be pounced on 7 and it certainly made fun of to an extraordinary extent by Mr, Harry Tote's Com- pany. Nevt.stheless, it is uproariously amusing. The idea is that the owner of a flying machine pro- fesses to have invented a machine the like of which the world has never seen before. But high anticipa- tions fall a little whoa flying wonder "collapses Just before Marling on is longest journey. With each a " turn "in dm bill the other itema look a little more commonplace ; but nevertheless there are clever and amusing " turns " ad. lib. There are Les Croieits, three remarkably clever acrobats of the heavy " tyce, who accomplieh a series of novel acrobatical tricks without the aid of all the paraphernalia with which acrobats usually provide themselves with. The Croiaite apparently are not dependent on any apparatus except their own bodies,, and their versatile exhibition includes some wonderful balancing, and feats of strength. We have more acro- batics, this time mixed with • little humorous tumbling in The Falcone, who indulge in some amusing antics. They mix comedy with real cleverness in an attrac- tive manner. The remainder of the programme is fair. Irene and Morton are quite clever in con- , versational comedy and novelty dancing, whilst Sam ' Williams, vocal comedian, contributes some original siege in an excellent style. Other" turns "arc J. A. Wilson, comedian and dancer, in a sketch entitled 'lle troubles of' a recruiting eergeant." Nora Deemond, American picture artiste ; and Calm and Morrie, comedy artistes. A feature of the programme is the playing of the orchestra under the directioa of Mr. Widdop, the selection " Menetier de st Wesel" (Ilorldien) being rendered in an erpecially tasteful manner. Perfection pictures are aleo shown.</p>
