        <p>Ilb•--, INDEPENDENT ORDER OF RECHABITES.</p>
        <p>Warrington Juvenile District No. 4.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting was held in the Wycliffe Memorial Hall, on Saturday last, when there were present—the Superintendent, Bro. B.Walker; Resisted by the whole of the officers end 15 juvenile tent representatives. The SY.CRETARY, Bro. T. Masser, gave hie report which showed 94 new members had been admitted in the lam quarter of the year and after having transferred to adult tents the total memhership stood at 1394. The funds amounted to .£521 Ils. 4d. The Secretary also reported that the tent to win the Devil and Block for the beat percentage of membership for the year 1910 was Hope of Sankey tent with 69 per cent. The balance .beet and auditor's report were given and acoepted. The SITKRINTENDENT gave his report of several •isits to tents several of which he had the pleasure of presenting prises for attendance, etc. Bro.W.Wniorur also gave bis report of a meeting held at Damen under the Lancashire and Cheshire Band of IVe elUnegai7i.on of ofhoer. then took place, and resulted as follows :—District superintendent, Bro. C. S. Fellow.; DS., Bro. Barker ; Levite, Bro. Pendleton ; Guardian, Bro. P. froward ; trustee., Bros. Horne, Sutton, Lawton ; secretary, Bro. T. M assay ; treasurer, Bro. Ratendale ; P. Superintendent, Bro. B. Walker. Representative. to Lancashire and Cheshire 8.0. H. Union, Bros. P. Howard and C. Pewney ; Press cornea. pondenta Bro.. F. Ingham and Pendleton ; auditors, Bros. Wright and 1101. It was decided to make a presentation to Bro. S. 6ailop for his services in going through the offices of the districtilt WllB also agreed to bold the Dint meeting at Orrall.</p>
        <p>lea was provided by the friends at Wycliffe, for which they wore heartily thanked.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-04_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>OUR SERIAL STORY.</head>
        <p>OUR SERIAL STORY.</p>
        <p>THE MYSTERY OF ROGER BULLOCK,</p>
        <p>TOM GALLON (Author of " Tatterley," " Meg the Lady," etc.).</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XII,</p>
        <p>WITHERS' WAY.</p>
        <p>In a mean little lodging in Conrad Street, Lambeth, a woman stood at the window of a room, looking out on to the squalid street, awl waiting. The woman won Lydia Pardon, and she had waited in that way for what seemed en eternity—waited with a craving hunger in her to be at the throat of the man who had killed Roger Bit:lock. She had con- fided in that strange little man, Robert Withers; she knew, °VIM] while she chafed and fretted at the delay, that he was work- ing secretly towards th'e same end. Meknes:4 was falling swiftly, and io turned away at last teem the window, and proceeded to light her lamp. As she did so there came a quick kncek on the panels of the door, and as she spoke in answer to it, the door was opened, and the mau about whom she had been thinking came in quick- ly. lie was followed by a younger man, whose face. she seemed to kn</p>
        <p>" Well ?" she ached riTu. Mr. Withers placed his het on the table. and fitrmally Amok hands with her. "On the track at last," he said, in his precise way. "We start in half an hour for Wrath. ergate; are you prepared to come with us "On that errand—l'm prepared to come to the end of the world," site answered. She stood looking curiously at the younger man and Withers in his abrupt fashion intco duced them.</p>
        <p>"'l'his iS Mr. Connaway—and although you haven't met before, in ,the usual sense you've been pretty do.e together more than once," ho said whimsically. "This is the man who wan with our friend Jarrow in the mill."</p>
        <p>" The younger of the two men ?" she ex- claimed quickly, her fao• hardening a littlo "But how do you know—" She left the sentence unfinished; Wither, finished it quickly for her. How do I know that thin is not the mall we want ?" he saki. "Because I've worked out, through different conclusions, the intricate busine4 for mYeelf ; and no far an I can wee out friend hero stands c tear. It's the other man we want; but we'vo got to get him. Ile's cunning fellow, and I don't how yet quite how to deal with him if we catch him. But I've a plan in my mind for wr nging a con. tension out of him —and I think I shall nun need."</p>
        <p>--ttihat do you twant me to do ?" nhe de- manded.</p>
        <p>"I know that 'this man Jarrow is still down at Weatlre-gate—and in hiding. I have reason to low that—a reason which I will explain to you presently. Is it possible for you to find 'LIM io it posaiblo for you to induce him, by any means—by threats or persuasion—to goi bark to Ferry House ?" " Why should- he do that—what. purpose would it serve she asked in astonishment.</p>
        <p>" That's my nffair ; you know you pro. miaed to leave the matter in my hands," he retorted. " fre scheme in my head is one that can only be worked out in a certain way ; but te.morrow night I must get Neal Jarrow back to tho mill."</p>
        <p>"I think L begin to understand," said Ly- dia, nodding slowly. You purpose catching him there. rind conf ronti ng ham with the body of 'Roger Bullock ?" Mr. Withirs omiled. You're a cute wo- man," No said; " but to that I can only an. swer wqh a yes and a no. Yes—becauso it's part of my plan to catch him there; no— because I mail confront him with what isn't there."</p>
        <p>"Do you mean that the body is gone the del-landed breathlessly. "I mean thlt the body is buried away, and hidden from the sight of men; and Neal Jarrow helped to bury it," said the little man solem-dy. "Save by the inereat chance It wt)lnever be found.'</p>
        <p>In the most painstaking way Mr. Withers proceeded tib tell them of his adventures, and those df Richard Bullock, during the past two or tltree days. Of how he had actu- ally gone to the mil', and seen the body of Roger Bullock; of how, on going back in the hope to get the keys of the rooms in Cloister Chamners, he had found the body gone; of the -natural guess on his part that Jarrow had do apped it into the river. Then he told them 1 ow Richard Bullock had been present at tine latter part of that strange burial under IMe floor of the disused stable; and of the flht of Neal Jarrow on seeing him.</p>
        <p>" The man !las got it into his head that the spirit of Roger Bullock walks the earth, • he said in conclusion. "lie has never seen the other brother and does not know of his existence. On that I mean to trade; by means of that I mean, if possible, to force a confession from him."</p>
        <p>_ . " But why do this, now that Roger But- Lock is hidden away ?" *irk asked. " For a double reason," answered the little fisherman. "First. if possible, to bring a scoundrel to justice; seCondly, to satisfy myself. Remember that there fs al- ways the possibility, my dear Connaway, that the murder may be discovered; in that case you aro no longer safe. You are known to have been in that place; you have actual- ly made a confession of the crime; what chance would you stand with a plausible rascal like Jarrow. But come," he added, looking at his watch-- You have no time to logo if were to reach Weathergate to- night." In a few minutes the three had started from Conrad Street, and were Oil their way to the station. In the train Mark sat in one corner; filled with his own thoughts; it seemed strange to think that but a few hours before he had been talking to Monte- line—and now she had gone out of his life. That ever-present gnawing anxiety in re- gard to his future was with him now, more strongly than ever; it obliterated from his mind the errand upon which they were go- he little fisherman at the other end of the compartment, was leaning forward, talk- ing to Lydia Pardon, and emphasising some instructions strongly; the woman, with a set face, nodded from time to time her un- derstanding of those instructions. 'this was, in a sense, the crisis of Mr. Withers life, and he was not going to let any misadven- tures spoil it. . . .</p>
        <p>-Solt they came to Weathergate, there, nutaide the .tation, the three se</p>
        <p>ated—the two men going in ono di and the woman in another. That seemi</p>
        <p>fin—tl;S—reSnir,;( 'seine understanding lu herself and Withers; for she went off,</p>
        <p>out a word.</p>
        <p>_ " She knows what she's doing,' Withers, as he took Mark by th</p>
        <p>She a got a ce in rta t eory a a and how and where she ran find himd</p>
        <p>while, we have to watt the coming ard Bullock."</p>
        <p>"To he coming down here ?" nekl his heart leaping. ' ".11e 140's vl</p>
        <p>trt of the senernt,,— sant thC"Other. But !Mall wire him early in the morning, and' know he'll come down. Part of the scheme,</p>
        <p>my friend—part of the great scheme." they went off together to the Fisherman's Arms, and Mr. Withers ordered supper. The landlady looked curiously at Mark, when presently she happened to come into the room: but she made no comment on the fact that he had been there before. Mark, for his part, found himself thinking again and again of that strange woman who had left them no abruptly that light, and had started off to find Neal Jarrow. Ile spoke of her when he finally shook hands with Mr : Withers rt the door of th• tatters bedroom.</p>
        <p>Do- you 'think she'll find him ?" " My char boy," said the little fisherman cheerfully. "have you ever seen the meth- ods of a bloodhound ? That intelligent beast follows a trail that no one else ran see— follows on and on, until at last it pulls . . . „„</p>
        <p>down to victim. That woman is on suili a</p>
        <p>trail now—and in the end ahe'll pull down Jarrow. mark my words she's doing it for the Koko of • worthless man she loved. flood. night !" Lydia Pardon was indeed at work. There was no thought of sleep for her; ohs had bought some food, with money Withers had given her—but that she carried with her. She flitted like a grey shadow through the old town of Weathergate. looking about her sharply all the time, and yet without seem. lug to turn her head; no figure that went past her escaped her notice. So she came to : the house of Jabez hooch, and saw that it was all shut up and dark; she vanished round the back of it, and came to that , door in the wall at the end of the grounds. There she waited.</p>
        <p>Presently that door opened cautiously • little way, anti a face looked out then the door we., closed again. The woman gave a little short laugh in her throat at the accuracy of her reckoning; for the fare had heen that of Neal Jarrow. She changed her position againat the wall, no as to get the other side of the door; she knew that when the man came out, as he must pre- sently, he would turn in the opposite direc- tion, and she could fallow him.</p>
        <p>- In another minute the door opened again, and Jarrow glided out, and closed it. She knew what he had been to nee: knew that, with the uneasy conscience of the murderer, he hail been to look upon that grave—to be certain that it did not look auspicious—or that no one had disturbed it—or that it was exactly as it had been left by Roach and himself. And sho knew that while ever he remained anywhere near Weathergate, no be would .MID back, again and again, to that place, fearful of what might have hap- pened in his absence. Ile walked away quickly and the woman followed. For a long time he did not notice her; but when he did lie stopped, and looked bark at her; then, as if uncertain whethn she was following him or not, went on again. Finding that she kept that distance between them, and stopped when he stopped, and went on when he went on, he presently turned, and walked back towarda her. " Lydia Pardon !" he stammered. "What are von doing here F" " What are you doing here ?" she retort- ed. " I'vo come down to find Roger Bul- lock." The man looked at her for a moment or two as though not understanding. "You won't find him here," he said at last. "flea --he's gone away." She shrok her head at him, and laughed— a weird laugh that startled him. "lie hasn't gone away; he's down here still," she an- swered, lowering her voice. "Ito the strangest thing in the world. I heard it whispered that he was dead—but that isn't true; he walks about just the same on ever among men. I now him near the old mill that they call the Ferry House this .very night." It's a lie ! You're mad, Lydia !" he cried, raising his hands for a moment as though he would strike her. "Ito impossi- ble."</p>
        <p>"But haven't you seen him yourself— within the past day or two ?" persisted the woman. "Think !"</p>
        <p>"It's curious," he said, staring at her horribly. " And yet I thought so myself. When I was in London the other day I saw his name on the book in the hall of it club— I knew the writing perfectly. Then the por ter at Ma chambers told me he wee there— upstarrs in his rooms." "I saw him there myself," she broke in quickly—"l talked with him there." He moved a step or two away from her. As though he dared not let her see his face; controlled himself, and came back again. Be patient with me," he said. "Tell me the date and the day you saw him last at his chambers."</p>
        <p>(larefully she told him what the date and the day had been; the wretched man stood before her, pressing his hands to his eyes and striving to think. 'After he had stood like that for a moment or two, she asked him quietly—" Is anythieg the matter, Mr. Jarrow ?" Ile lowered his hands, and showed his white-drawn face. "No—no—nothing is the matter," he said. "I have not been well lately. Good-night. ' He set off at a great rate, and once more she followed him. For a long time he did not seem to notice her, and when he did at last they had come to the border of the marshes outside the town. He turned upon her then furiously, and demanded to know why she followed him.</p>
        <p>" I am looking for Roger Bullock," she answered him patiently. He went on again, and again she followed. They came at last near to the old mill, standing up gaunt and ugly against the night shy; and the man stood for a long time looking at the place. Presently he turned away, and coming to a bank at the side of the road, sat down there, with his head in his hands, to rest. The woman sat not far from him, and watched him. Some half hour later he came up to her, and touched her on the shoulder. She started, and looked up. " I didn't quite understand you," he mid in a low voice. "Did you nay that you law —saw Roger Bullock near the mill to- night ?" She nodded. "I aaw him distinctly." ehe said. " And ha left a message for you—l was Idt o tell you if I saw you." could not know that she was lying; he co not know her purpose. Ile steed there, a ring at her; his lips were twitching. • Ile spoke to you? Ile gave you a mess- g::-.1--(feoar,,, me wa:-."01:: stammered. rep. reply. "lie said he would ant you in the old mill to-morrow night at o clock," she raid steadily. Then, with. king at the man again, she turned and walked away, and was lost in ness. Jarrow stood in the road, 'star- r her, and with his brain reeling. although Lydia Pardon, in passing of Jobe, Roach, had seen no light a Roach was hard at work in room where the great desk woo; taken the precaution to close and shotters. It seenital that thitre work to be done, and no great epees covered not only the desk he door. Some of these had been many were half consumed in the re 3 small fire burned. The big pen, and Roach kept trotting and forwards between that and rting the, yore,. 1,3 t.,, • 71Ild et-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-04_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>In the FLAVOUR that the</head>
        <p>In the FLAVOUR that the</p>
        <p>- w are</p>
        <p>favour-</p>
        <p>LECT Cocoa</p>
        <p>'ebuary74, 1911.</p>
        <p>',undies IH heaps, On the floor wan an old leather portmanteau, open, and with a few articles of clothing in it; into this from "lime to time he dropped a bundle of papers, until it wan rapidly be- coming full. In the midst of his ..cenpation Lazarus Iles entered, and stood looking at !Jim.</p>
        <p>"Well, Iles—it's come to the parting of the wa,vs—hasn't it ?“ said the old man. "I was never one for sentiment, as you know, but we've been together a good many years, and I'm sorry for your sake that I've got to turn You out."</p>
        <p>What country are you making for ?" asked Lazarus.</p>
        <p>Mr. Roach put his finger to the side of his near, and closed oee eye. " That's tell- ing, Lazarus," he said with a grin. " Never you mind where I'm going; I shall be able to make a little nest for myself somewhere or other. I shall be able La line it well, too," he added, pointing to the bundles of papers in the portmanteau. "I've done rather well for myself, Lazarus, all things considered."</p>
        <p>--t7iii;s—inadeline's fortune, I attPln.o auggested Iles. " 'the fortune I WBB to share in, he added bitterly. " You weren't sharp enough. Laz-irtm, as I told you beton, you're no good, except as a clerk. Yes—that's Madeline's fortune— all good securities that can lie realised: all comfortable securities. Solid money, Lazar- na—and thousands of it. Added to which thorn are other things, screwed out of other people; all little comforts for a poor old inon in his declining years. There are places in South America," added the old man meditatively—" places where the sun shines, and where one can be quite happy, if one has a fortune and no questions are asked. Not that I'm telling you that I'm going to South America, Lazarus," he added slyly— " because I'm not. I haven't decided yet what spot on earth I shall choose. But I'm off to-night. ' " And I must turn out of this place, and make a start somewhere else, I suppose," said Iles. "Suppose I turn informer; sup- pose, before you can get clear of England. I set this Richard Bullock on your track, and you're caught—what then "If I thought you'd do that, Lazarus, said the old man with the utmost cheerful- ness, "I'd roe to it that you were not in a condition to talk to anybody, or to tell talc, Knowing you're only joking, my dear , Lazarus, I won't' take you seriously. Re.ddes, in any case you've got to find out where I am, and I might lie in hiding in Ragland quite a king time. I'm a deep dog, and a sly one, Lazarus; you won't catch me easi- ly." 'the old man went on again with Me work; presently he looked up with a startled face. "Is that my fancy—or is thltre SOMCOIIO knocking ?" he asked. "It struck me just now that there wan aconeone knocking quietly at the dime," said Lazarus.. "Yea, there it is again." ..</p>
        <p>" to that end room 'Were there's no light, and open the window softly, and look out," Roach directed him. " See who it is, and come hark and tell me." The knocking continued at intervals— timidly, as though the person knocking was half afraid to venture. That wax explained when in a minute or two Lazarus Iles came back into the room.</p>
        <p>'• It's Mrs. Roach," he said. Julies Roach leant against the desk, with his hands upon it, and looked down at the portmanteau which contained that ill-gotten fortune of his. bike a persistent chorus to his thoughts the knocking went on softly and intermittently, whim Lazarus. Iles watched his master, with a dumb enquiry in his eyes as to what he should do. At last the old man broke out passionately. From the first she has been in the way," he exclaimed—" from the very first. I was • fool when I married her ; she's been a drag on me ever since. I only married her because I thought she had money—and in that I was cheated."</p>
        <p>" She can't knock there all night," Iles reminded him.</p>
        <p>"Go down, and let her in," Roach com- manded. "And mind—not a word to her. Simply bring her up here." Lazarus Ilea disappeared, and there was heard below the opening and the shutting of o door. Susan Roach, coming in timidly, clutched Lazarus by the arm, and whispered a nervous question. " Will he see me?"</p>
        <p>"Yen—ho sent me .down to let you in," said the man. "Between ourseises, Mrs. Roach," he whispered hoarsely, "Bosch is making a bolt of it to-night—clearing out for good and all. Humour him, Mrs. Roach; things may be smoother than you think." They went upstairs togetlier—the man first and the woman following. Iles opened the door of the room, and Susan Roach went in, to find her husband standing by the great desk in the disordered room, and look- ing at her narrowly out of his cunning eyes. " Well, Mrs. Roach," was his greeting— "so you've come back7eh ?" •</p>
        <p>"I woo afraid when I ran away," she an- swered. "I was afraid of what I had done. Hut ever since then, while I've been wander- ing about, I've wanted to come back to you; I've wanted to tell you that I never meant to do you any harm: I only wanted to save you from yourself, in a manner of speaking. You have hasty passions, John; you are easily carried away when things don't go your way. I hope you'll forgive me, dear % Mr. Roach threw back his head, and burst into a peal of uncanny laughter— laughter no horrible and no prolonged that his two companions stared at him in amase- ment. At last he recovered himself, and turned to the woman, and spoke with a deadly calmness that should have warned her to look below the surface of it.</p>
        <p>" And what do you want now, Mrs. Roach he asked.</p>
        <p>If you're going away, Jabez—away from this dreadful house where I have been to unhappy—take me with you," she plead- ed. "I would never be in the way, and I might be able to help you, and to look after you. You're an old man. Jabes, and the time to coining when you may be ill and weak, and need me with you. Let me go with you, labez; I cost no little and need on little." "Yin, Mrs. Roach—l am going away," snid Jabes, nodding his bead at her many times. "fm going out into the world, and I'm going to enjoy myself. Naturally I should want you with me—you who have always been so loyal to me, and have al- ways had my best interests at heart. Stupid of me not to have thought of that before. Certainly, Mrs. Roach,—you shall travel with me."</p>
        <p>"Thank you, Jabez ; it is kind of you to have forgiven me for any dislovalty to you," she murmured humbly, "But 'that was only done for your sake." . . . . . .</p>
        <p>Jahem-le,ach drove his hands deep into him pocketm, and pulled out a handful of coil.. ' You'll need to be paid your wagea, Lan- arum Iles—for the last time," maid the old man. "I think I've reckoned it right," he added, eounting out some gold and silver on the desk.</p>
        <p>Lazarus Iles picked it up, counted it, and transferred it to his own pocket. "Is this the end then?" he &amp;sired.</p>
        <p>" This in the end," said Roach, looking not at him, but at the woman. " You can MN. Roach, apparently utterly worn out, had seated herself in a chair at the further end of the room; the two men were to- gether at the door for a moment. There, as Lazarus Iles paused, he asked a question of his late master.</p>
        <p>' \Nina are you going to do with her " I'm going to be very kind and gentle with her," murmured the old man. " It's no busiscoot yours; take whatever belongs tr YOU, and go." Whatever belonged to Lazarua Iles ap- peared to he contained in a large brown pap, pari.tili with that under onc arm he walked ~f none Mr.</p>
        <p>noaeo rostenen tile outer nom% sow wallet-, up the Ktairs slowly to the room in which he had left hie wife.</p>
        <p>"How far are we going, Jahn was her first question. " How far I'm going I don't exactly know," he replied. "So far as you're con- cerned, vour journey ends here:' " \Thai do von mean ?" she asked start led: . • .</p>
        <p>Mr. Roach opened a corner cuplanird, and took out a bottle and a glans; half filled the glans, and drank it oft. " You sent that girl away from this house—and young Connaway with her. be said. "In all my life, what- ever I've done has been spoilt by you, and thwarted by you ; well pot an end to that. In this bag "—he kicked it with his foot as he spoke—" is a fortune I'm going to poky at the other side of the world. But. I'll I. troubled with no such woman as lOU ; going to leave you behind." " lobes !" she exclaimed, starting to her feet.</p>
        <p>You crept in here to-night under corer of darkneer: you won t be seen again. knees is a place under this house where you can he safely hidden: a .lace where you can scream your heart on, if you like, and no one will hear you. There you shall atop ; there 11l leave you, until such time ann you starve to death, or the rata get at you. Think of it, my sweet Susan." lie went on, advancing to- wards her-.' think of lying in the dark— locked in—with the roaring, rushing water for ever sounding in your cars, until death comes mercifully, to put an end to your auf- feringn. I've not much time to lose; I must get out of this place, and get away. Came with me, you jade !" She struggled strongly with him; on her knee, at his feet begged and implored that he would let her gi, and that he would re- member years when they both had ',Ca yoanger—aimve all, that he would remem- ber vows that he had made to her. Ile took no notice of all that ; he half dragged and half carried her down the stairs to that great door leading into the cellars, she im- ploring him all the way, viii striving to kiss his hands, and even his lips. half fainting at last, she sank down against the d.sir, while he unlocked it, and threw it open. Ile dragged her into the place, and kft her for a moment lying on the stones, white he groped about to find a candle he knew was there. The roaring of the water had by this time deadened the aound of her plead- ing', ; Imd deadened also the 1101111,1 of his cursing that he could not find the candle. While sim lay there moaning she heard a sudden shriek from him, us h. stumbl,l over the stone that had heen kid upon the floor; heard a scream V.. 1 lie plunged head first into that ininare hole in the floor. She thought she heard an inarticulate cry : th-lt all wan still, save for that roaring if the water below.</p>
        <p>After what seemed a long lime she begor. to grope her away about the floor, and so at last stumbled upon a candl,tiek. Tremb- lingly and with difficulty ie got a light, and lit the candle; looked about her, and I,ON 01111 011,11 Spare ifl iho floor ; peered down, with the candle in her hand, and saw the snaking water beneath. But of Jab,z she saw nothing; the water had carried him away under the town. Presently when some strength hod come haek to her, she did a curio's,. thing. After kneeling beside that place for quite a long time, with her face hidden in her hands, she got lip. and with a strength one would never have stepected her of posse.dng, raised the great atone inch by inch, until at last she had got it net up on edge; then with a mighty Phenol she flung it forward over the hole, and saw it sink into place. Laughing hysterically, she caught up the candle, and staggered out of the place; locked the dear with the key Jabra had dropped, and ran up through the house again. She extin- guished all the lights, and coming at last to the big hall door, opened it—went out swift- ly into tho street —and disappeared.</p>
        <p>(To be Coocluded.)</p>
