        <p>BADEN POWELL BOY SCOUTS.</p>
        <p>--- The Progrus In Warrington.</p>
        <p>The Roy Scout Organisation inaugurated by Sir Robert linden-Powell, was intended to auppliment existing boy, organisation., and to indicate bow troops and patrols might. te formed which would be unattached ,to any of these. The movement ha spread so tepidly that it was necessary to bring its plan into use througboitt the country, to ensure inifortnity on. broad basis. To aught this • headquarters council ham been formed under the leadership ot Lietit-lieneral Sir Rob. t S. S. Baden-Powell, I,C.Jt , tho chief scout, to whom the general management of the move- ment has been entrusted, and under his supervision • wheme should be formulated for general guidance. The first step is in the formation of local amoolations, in nay centre of population to be c,allod a boy scout association, to which gentlemen interested in work , among boys abould be invited. I In Warrington an association wen formed about two years ago, at • meeting held 'indente auspices of Major Strachan, • cornsiostoner appointed from the bend- quarters in London, but owing to the overwhelming work involved in organising NO large • county an Lanonehire, slow progress has been made in defining chariots ...liable mares for controlling the V. 1601.10 troops sod patrols. A temporary arrtingement 'e.. come 'o that Warrington be a centre of the district comprimed in a radios of eight or ten mil.. I Tbe area thus denned proved unwieldly sod sub-sections I have been formed in Wigan, St.klelens,Lyrom,Mc. The Werrington area now includes within its limits Stockton Heath. Grappenhall and a large portion of the Newton Parliametaary Division. The boys scoots scheme is for boys of every denomination and creed, thin floes not pmt.'s the raising and formation of troops by any recognined existing organisation. Such troops are affiliated to the local scout association, and agree to conform generally to the principles laid down in the hand book "Scouting for boys." Lieut.-Col. J. D. Buckton, V.D., has been appointed district commissioner tinder the county comnitsatoner by warrant signed by the chief scout. His ditties are (a) to in•pect troops and patrols (13) to test bodge wearers in their knowledge of their .objects. (c) to acme the harmoniouu no-operation of ull the mooch. lions and scoot masters in the district (d) To be the concurring authority for the recommendation of local associations for the inan and withdrawal of scout- masters warrants before transmission to headquarters. (e) To foster and encourage the moverneat generally , throughout the district. Scout-masters end assistant, scontaamters are nomi- nated by the local Association and recommendmi by the district commissioner to receive warrants by the chief scout after serving three months probation with • troop. Badges ate granted by the asinsintion to boy scouts who have pawed certain tilts of proficiency on the recommendation of scoutmaaters. lhe use of titles ' and terns denoting military or nasal rank such an captain. Lieutenant, commander, etc., are not authoriend nod should not be used. This brief abstract may serve to bring before the general public sonata the leading 1 points laid down by the chief scout In his moellent handbook, to which we would draw the attention of all interested in boys and their management during • critical period of life when they •re beginning to think nod not .for themselves.</p>
        <p>Ilia Warship the Mayor (Councillor IS. Joseph) takes • warm intereet in the movement in Warrington and has called a public meeting to be held in the Om Hall on Thur•day, 9th of February, at 7.30 p.m. when • demonstration will he given by the boy scouts who will afterwards be entertained to a hot-pet supper. While • the boys are enjoying the good things provided by the Mayor in the smaller hall the audience will be addressed by several speakers in advocacy of the cense and an appeal made for financial support. The expenses op to the present have been met by a few gentlemen along with funds earned by the scouts ' themreives, by their work and not by begging. Ike great aim of the chief scout is solely to make the rising generation into good citizens by creating • desire in the lad to forget himself, and developb character which I. essential for making hie wvy in life. The boy scents code of homier is " I promise that twill do my best (I) To do any duty to God and the King ; (2) To help other people at all these; (3) To obey the Scout Law."</p>
        <p>ON&amp; &amp; ALL GARDENING, 1911. Edhad by EDWARD OWZR 011f12111.0, Y.K.H.S., Loudon : Agricultural sad Horticultural Amemation. Price twopeous. _</p>
        <p>• The sixteenth year of this interesting annual Undo it Intl of Ws and vigour and as popular as ever with lovers of gardens, for whom the publishers have provided a first edition of Ifet,aoo copies. The editor devotes his opening •rticle LO 11 comprehensive plan for developing the work of existing ilortictiltoral Societies throughout the kingdom. Them societies he estimates at a thousand in somber, with ettarter of a million members whom hie would convert into missionaries for the converainn tf our coontry into on " Garden Land." The in rag., of the minuet are a nasal foil of instructive article, anti pleasant illus. UMW..</p>
