        <p>For manv years the Ile, J. E. listtenliory has paid vigils to radio. Wesleyan Church lind on each occasion he hva received the cordial welcome which is the doe of one who ho attained sook high recognition in the couneels of Wesleyan :Methodism. This year the visit took place on Wednesday and,es ilimal,the rev. gentleman preached an the afterno n and in the even- ing delivered one of those lectures hich makes strong spriest to his hearers. . . . . . .</p>
        <p>The church am crowded when the evening's pro- ceedings commenced. Mr. George Weever presided and 'lmporting him on the dais,beside t he lecturer,were the Item. Thomas Glodding and Or. Bennett. The Chairntan remarked that Ito watt exceedingly glad that the subject tor the evening seas to he" 111.6 Price Hughes," whom be considered to lie one of the great figure, of the close of the 19th centrry. lie was • man full of pemionate zeal and anything thnt was for the wellbeing of the people he never hesitated to support. (Applaumi. The Lecturer at the outset remarked that he could ' of Mcflughes as e eury prejudiced admirer. ‘llnely '/k did not make the least attempt to talk shout bite in an unbiassed way. He did not believe in unbiassed lemons and he frankly said that he was not one himgelf. N hen he spoke about I lugh Price Hugh.en he spoke of one ssho wits one of the chief heroes of his childhood and who was one of the aspirations of his manhood.He lot ed and honoured hint as he loved and honoured no other puttlic mutt and, therefore, if they expected an unbiassed opinion of hint they would not got it that evening. He considered him to have been altogether the greetest gift of God to Methodism and through Methodism to his country niece the time of John Wesley. (Appleme.) Mr. Hughes, the lecturer con- tinued, sass good moldier of Jesus Christ, en Res n man who never turned his beck, but warched forward, never doubting that the clouds would break. He herb a Methodist ancestry, his grandfather being the first miniater to be elected to the legal conference. His father assn Welsh num and he won born in • Weleh town. From his earliest years he exhibited a tem- pestuous temper and some of the erdoor of the social reformer. A typicel inetauca of that was found in the fact that when hi, norm wee just married and they were " towing " her iti champagne he knocked the glass out , of her hands and soil he would not hove "hie dear old nurse drink that stuff." (Laughter.) In Lis school- days his personality was no vivid that its colonel were noon displayed. After much heertsearching and trouble • Mr. trochee, when shod of 1-1 yearn, cave himself to Jenny Christ and immediately he wanted to preach—he wee nothing if not precocious. He did n, net the superintendent to put him oath, plan—ho knew super. intended,. Ito well—but he at once hired a cottage and contrived to pet a number of old women together and then he preached to them. He goon came to be known as the boy preacher. At a very early age be tweet to Hichtuotol Culleee and like all sennible people begen lite ass hero. (Lltighter.i That was how all sensible life;people hegas he would not my how they ended it. (More laughter., 118 Was twice expelled from college and if it had not been for one of the niasters Dr. !sleeken/ his name would have been written in that of another denorningtion. Ike college regulntions were too wrict for him, and it ear not long before he had some of them altered. When he left college he was a mry euperior young man—most people eere when they left college, but he was more so than most others. (I...tighter.) He alweye emphasised everything he did , —good or bad—he could not help being el Oat M. At that time he had a great contempt for eredgelier, it being get erally felt then that if you cottid not pass examinatiims you might •t 18•8 t try to save eon's. by the infinite mercy of God, at the first famine h pr•tched et in Dover (his Dian circuit) a dozen people Rave themselves to Ood,and from that day he 3,ecaree a , firm Evengelist. (Addl..). It hal been sail thst he ' en, the greys., evangelist Meth, diem had bed, anti multitedea most have liven led to awl, through Mr. !Liebe,. influence. Continuing, Mr. Itatlenbury said that it can while at Sexton Hill that Mr litighes started the " Mothi,d int Time,," sail at first some people called it "The Loy'. Own Paper" liut it am neverthe- less a paper that wee "Hughes' full.'' (Laughter.) Tr.* West London Nlisaiort