        <p>Sr. HELENS FINANCE.</p>
        <p>CORPORATE OVERSPENDINGS.</p>
        <p>Tbe Finance Committee having n number of accounts for public improvements which bare been overspent. have decided to ask the Local Government Board•s perniiasion to borrow ,C 2,414 overspent on the Warring- ton now road and bridge improvenrent, Cowley School playing fields, and other works. Application is also to he made for leave to borrow £3,701 for new work. of Street impro•ernent. The Local Government Board ban already sanctioned a loan of £ri,736 for building Cowley Secondary School. for Boys, the period of repayment being fifty years. The Electricity COM- MARre have decided to apply for leave to borrow .€10,300 for extending the electricity works, which have been taxed for some time for large suppliee of elec- tricity by overhead wires to tire local works and collieries. Si'. HELENS TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>GAS OFFICES A DISGRACE TO TilE TOWN.</p>
        <p>The Mayor (Alderman J. Beecham) prottided at the mhnthly meeting of the St. (MMus Town Council on Wednesday. Mr. Crooks moved the minutes of the Gm Committee and drew attention to the proposal of the committee to pull down the existing ahowroorus and erect new offices at a cost of £2,100, exclusive of the cost of making proper foundations Mr T Walsh seconded, and in the course of consider- able discussion Mr Grace mid that anyone who ser.ot , to do business with the Gas Department roi St be struck by the perfectly horrible conditions under which the clerks and others had to work. The premier, were insanitary, and, if they had been in private ownership, might prohatily ham been condemned. The Mayor Mid he thought personally the One Com- mittee was doing the right thing in building the ollices. Ile said twenty years ago, when he was a member of , the Gm Committee, that if the place bail belonged to ' him he wool(' have swept it sway. It was a disgrace to the Corporation to have its clerks employed there. The minutes were then passed. Mr. Collier asked if the Parliamentary Committee had considered the suggested change in the Consult' street tram tertnirms.—Mr. W. Bates asked if the com- mittee hid decided to provide a silk banner with the borough cost of ores for the Turin Exhibition.— Alderman Forster silt] he was taking stops to have good one made, and the committee would do what they could to advertim the town in that way. The corn- 'motes had not yet discussed the question of the tra,tuway.terrniolli. . . . . .</p>
        <p>The minutes were rmssed and the meeting concluded.</p>
