      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-04_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>BOY'S TERRIBLE DEATH.</head>
        <p>BOY'S TERRIBLE DEATH.</p>
        <p>Bored by Red Hot Iron.</p>
        <p>A terrible accident occurred at the Ironworks of Pearson and Knowles Co., Intllsm, on Thursday morning.. . .</p>
        <p>A youth named Charles For, aged 16. residing with his parents at 13. Ifeeth-lane,War riugton.was at work in the Seven Inch mill at four o'clock in the morning, engaged.. in passing red hot email iron, almost approaching wire, through the rolls, when he apparently forgot that lie had sent a piece throligh, and a workman passing the iron back, the lad wee taken unawares, and the iron passed completely thiough the calf of his left leg. A workman who was standiog near drew the iron ro I out, causing the hoc excruiiating pain, and lie was conveyed to the Warrington Infirmary, where his injuries were attended to. The ~.hock of the frigtrifal • inunry, had, however, caused such a collap-e that Fox died shortly after his admission to the Institution. .-. . . .</p>
        <p>The iron., it is stated, was white hot. The tams have been communicated to the Coroner.</p>
      </div>
