        <p>Ur. J. A. Seddon Entertained, Mr. J. A. Serhlon. war on Monday evening enter- tained at dinner Py the Liverpool district council of the National Amalgamated Union of Shen Resistants. Watehoueernen. and Clerks. The function, which was 4f a most agreeable character, took place at the Bear's Paw Rertsurant, and was largely attended. The gue.t of the evening, in replying to the toast of hie health, expressed his deep sense of gratitude for the invitation and for the honour which the Liverpoool branch had conferred upon him. He also took the opportunity of friblicly expleseing the great loss the Shop Assistants' Union tied sustained hy the death of Sir Charter Nike. He of all oleo in the House of Commons had interested himself in their raise, and his death was a never. blow to the demands of the Shop Assistsnts' Union. (Hear. hear.) Referring to the work of organbathm among shop assistants, Mr. Seddon said the position toefay ma. much improved from that of ten year. ago. Slowly but sorely shop workers were beginning to recogoi-e the change in economic conditions, The aggregations of capital were creating a new wageeerning class, and if the shop workerr, were to secure anythi, 2 approximating to the conditions of labmr and tell:o,:i.ration whin other workers had, they must take up the same lines of organisation. (Applameet.</p>
