        <p>wolely. for the ltenet of the town, and its menthe's and any who know Warrington now and knew it £0 or 40 years ago, or in' Is:: I when the Society wait foundedxnust adroit that the society's work has borne rich !tuft, and thntthere are few towns which can show such a poi inimical record as Warrington. This re orrripg directly or indirectly to the somety's long continnedamd steady educational work. Now this beitt4 so it is surely lunch to be deplored that the society does not receive greater support and encourage:nem. There are now only 170 subscribers out of a population of 7:.01.0. giving an income which . in totally Inadequate to epahle the society to carry on. 41IM work succesafully and effectually, much less to 'twirl' 'other charitable institutions in the town as the Com- mittee would wish to do if adequately supported. The subscription rateii are very low indeed and out •'of all proportion to the excellence of the concerts and mrnnot but think that if it were thoroughly realized that the great pleasure, and profit which may he . . .</p>
