      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-04_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>Finance Act and Income Tax.</head>
        <p>The Finance Act and Income Tax.</p>
        <p>Sir,-1 leg to hand you copy of a letter which I have addressed to the Warrington Chamber of Commerce. anl which no doubt, will shortly be brought before a meeting of their Council. As the matter in one of public interest, I shall be glad if you would kindly insert the same in your next issue.—Yours faithfully. ......</p>
        <p>_ C. D. PARKINSON. 13eww,y Chatobern, Warringion, February let, 1911.</p>
        <p>Dear Mr. Bennett.— I ant afraid I ehall be unable to attend the animal meeting of your Chamber to be held this morning. There in, however, ens matter which should be brought to the notice of the Chamber, and that is the working of the present Finance Act in reference to income tax. Since the alteration was mule with regard to differentiation between uneatned and earned income (the latter, as you are aware, being charged at the rate of Oil, in the .0 where the total income does not exceed £200) per annum), many hardships have occurred which have been bronaht about in conse- quence of the strict administration of the law, of which law the income tax payer wan ignorant, and. owing to the fact that no special effort had been made to draw very particular attention to the new regulations. To state.l case or common occurrence—many such cases having come under my own notice. A trader earning nay .C4OO a year, who is able from his books to (womb tine Surveyor with such a atatement of profits :is meets with that otficial'e approval nod enables hint to make the aases.onent. The Surveyor then notifies the bailer of the assessment which has practically been mdtuallv agreed upon. :ind the receipt of this notice puts the trader's mind at rent. About the beginning of September he will receiie Form No. 38, which it is quite poesible—and in fact most likely—he will scarcely trouble to peruse, and will throw in the , waste-paper basket As being something which does snot apply to him, his assessineut having already been fixed. • If, however, he will carefully read the above lowed form, be will discover that a declaration is mon respect of his total income from t.d pa. t hC;. tp Zha'njeerVl:Yrao;toberethe e3oTi; ptember he will be "it of court, and will be , charged at the full rate of Is. 2d. on the sole of his income. The manner of stating the date on the inland Revenue fortn, viz "before the 30th September," is in knelt misleading. Many cases have occurred where the declaration ham been handed in to the Surveyor on the :filth September, hut has been refused as being too late, the 29th of that month being the last day on which claims will be admitted. In an ordinary legal damnient I submit that it would have been plainly set out that claims must be received on or" before the 29th September," and not " before the 30th September." The unfortunate trader, is therefore liable to be niulettsl in the payment of :al in the on his CICO more than he in legally asked to pay, through failing to make the declaration abuse referred to in the proper time, or from total ignorance of the necessity of such declaration, hating thrown the Form away. From !hose facts it must be apparent that, in common justice and fairness. tax-payers should be more distinctly warned in the first place of the absolute necessity of the declaration having to be completed and delivered, and sicondly, as to the exact date by which such declaration and claim must be rendered.</p>
        <p>-•</p>
        <p>Another inmem tent point that I should like to draw attention to is this. Wile, in eases within the know- ledge of the Surveyor, frotu the Returns made in previous tears, the tax gayer is entitled to be charged at the lower rate on earned Incoukebut hart faiied !rem some cause unknown for a space of ten days prior to the official dote to comply with the regulations a at:menial letter should be sent to him by the Sarveyor drawing his attention to the fact that his declaration and claims had not yet been received, and warning him of the penalty if the matter be not attended tn immediately. I would suggest that the various points mentioned in thin letter be brought before the Associated Chambers of Commerce, and if tl ey thought it was a fit matter to take up. that each separate chamber should be requested tm appioneh the renrective Member or Members of Parliament in their districts. and ask them to arrange for, and rapport a demand to be made in the House of Commons for a Covet n- ment Inquiry, with a mmcm to ascertaining time number of canoe where tax payers bare been compelled to pay more that what was legitimately mlue from theta on consequence of their inadvertently failing to comply with the methods of otlici al i sm.— I sm. yonrs sineerely, C. D. PARKINSON, Arthur Bennett. Esq.. Secretary, Warrington Chamber of Commerce.</p>
        <p>Linn° COMPO IND in a good Cough Mixture Iffecivis and Economical. Of Chemists ouly.—Adve</p>
        <p>p.</p>
