      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-04_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>MIDST TIIE RUINS OF THE STUCK-000M.</head>
        <p>MIDST TIIE RUINS OF THE STUCK-000M.</p>
        <p>, 4 diaastreua fire, resulting in the destrnetion ,of the warehouse and putt of the offices of the Richmond (ins move and leter : Company, Ltd, Academy-street, Warrington, occurred in the early hours of Thursday. At about 4.30 a night watehman named Flatten was going his round* when he saw flames i,tming from the top storey of the wareln,use. Ile promptly summoned the members of the works fire brigade, under the directjpon of Mr. C. F. Cot, but it was soon realized that tNfire had secured such a lirrn hold that they were unable to check it. Meanwhite no alarm was given at the Warrington fire station anti the borough brigade, in charge of Superintendent Turner, was quickly on the scene with a steam fire repine, escape, reel and 'Aden,. The flames made Pack rat id headway that the warehouse was now ablaze from end to end and another engine was sent for.</p>
        <p>After the fire had raged about half an hour, the roof fell in with a crult. The flames shot up to a Igreat ledght and the glare could be seen for a con- eiderahle distance. Whet. it was obvious that the warehouse was doomed, the brigade co, centrated their efforts on saving the workshops which adjoin ' the building. They were somewhat handicapped at the outset by the low pressure of the water aupnly, , :ind the been frost added to their difficulties. The yard, ladders, and btaircases were in an exceedingly slippery state, the water which spurted frotn the hoses as they played upon the burning building,</p>
        <p>being quickly frozen snil rendering it difficult for In teen to retain • te.huld. Superintendent Turner fell down a ataire.ise Inv fortunately escaped with nothing more serious than a few bruise..</p>
        <p>Mr. I Mutton., t manager) and Mr. J. W. Slack (secretary) were early on the scene and rendered useful assistance in ruperniaiiig operations. The three-storey building comprising the testing room, paint shop, and packing room. won completely gutted. When the fire had burnt itself out nothing remained but charred beams, twisted girders and piping, and a heap of stoves, &amp;a., most of which were battered and otherwise damaged to such an extent as to be useless. The general offices which almtted on the warehouse were also caught by the 'limes and partially destroyed. The more valuable ffice books had been removed the night previous into the safeaand these were intact Considering the close proximity of the building to other departments. the firemen effected a very commendable " save ' in confining the tire to the one block of property in which it originated. Two hours elapsed before the flames were got under ' control, but the brigade remained on the scene all morning. 'line tramway traffic on the Manchester-road route was held up until seven o'clock an the main line from the car shed in Mersey-street passes the works. . .</p>
        <p>The damage, which is covered by insurance iv estimated at between eSPOO and .£10,01.i0. Very little, if any, lops of employment will be caused. The warehouse and offices were gutted b,v fire in 1698. The cause of the outbreak is unknown.</p>
