        <p>Mr. C. l't.PA'sToN President of the Board of Agricultere) said the Conservative, did not attempt to defend the House of Loris as an ideal Chamber either front its past history or at the present time. Ile maitilnined. however, that during the lest few years. at any rate. its deliberations had been marked by a greater sense of responsibility and a far ke.ener senile of the interests of the Empire. They could only judge the efficacy or otherwise of Single Chamber Government In this country by the Home of Parliament in the Commonwealth Period, and all history condemned that as an absolute failure. It proved a Chamber of injustice, intolerance and tyrneny, so much to that the greatest of Noticonformieta, Crom- well, dissolved it for the salvation of hi. country. All nations on the face of the earth that were considered to be civilized refolded the two Cheniber system and ill new Constitutions were built upon that principle, the latest example being the union of South Africa. The latter wheme of government received the %section of the present Medical administration. The judgment of history, tire minore, of foreign c inntrien and of our own Colonies were all realist the single. Chamber system. Even if that were not so, we should 'hesitate to adopt the principle of single. Chamber gevernment, for we found that the tendency of the present day was to show lean consideration to the cpiniens and views of the di ITerent frictions in the house of Commons, and year by year the executi‘e was gaining in power. The members of the House were looked upon more m mere delegates titan as indi•ldnals r. ith opinionn of their own and if Vint tendency developed we should be ruled by the worst tenni of autocracy instead of on demo- cratic lines. If we had an ideal Home of Commons, perfectly representing the nation, always moderate, never passionate. ..roiling in men of Warne, never omitting the slow end steady form necesenry for good consideration, it ear possible that we should no