ith the whole of the Budget—(Government " hear, hear ")—althosigh it agreed with nine parts of it, was willing to swallow the whole Budget if ouly it could get the Home of Lordn out of the way. The same applied to Scotland, lint he was not sure about England. (Laughter.) Alter all. the English loved a lord. Laughter.) They had peculiar sentimental ideas of what a lord really wm. (Laughter.: Ile had no hesitation in saying that the House of Lords hail never jostilled its existence—et Any rate, not for tomes...rations. Oa the contrary, it hid justified its destruction. Ile could never forget the action of Lord Laos lowne and tome of hi, followers with regard to the Licensing 13ill. It was a disgrace and scandal that a measure on which months of tin. and thought had been expended should he saorificed in that way. 'the Liberals were unable to bring in 113 strong nieasurm MI they desired, for they knew they vroold not pam the Home of I, irds and the result was that' milk mud water " lichen)e, had sometimes to be introduced. The House of Lord, had always stood in the way of tnie social reforms mei it was that assembly which upheld nor wicked land system. The people of England would not listen to any scheme. of reform until the veto of the Lords had teen ah,lilhed- Mr. 1.. WARBURTON (First Commissioner of Works) said the preceding spanker had never so much as looked at the Bill under discussion. (Laughter.) The • last election proved heyond doubt that the majorit• rrf the people of England were in favour of the Con- . servative manure for the reform of the Home of Lords. ‘" No, no.") At arty rate, it proved that the Liberals were not to have things all their own say. When the Liberal Government in the Debating Society brought in a Bill to reform the House of Loris. they proposed that the Peers Mould hove the power to elect ISO rept...seer ativss, whereas the Conservatives proposed only 50. The first named Bill helped to drive its introducer over to the Conaer•ntive benches