        <p>Mr. W. Riot, (N. W. Belfast) said that before the introduction of the Referendum it we necenary that the whole of the institutions of the country should he placed on a democratic tees. And yet they found that the House of Lords would retain the hereditnry prin- ciple, with the addition of a nominated element, and that uo nortion of the Chlmliar vrould tie on an elective beim. Therefore, the seerne thoroughly undemo- cratic fuel unworthy to associnte with a measure relat- ing to the introduction of the Referendum. The Colonial Secretary did not trust the people no far ns to give them power to net the Referendum in motion. The House of Lords solely would hue the power to decide whether the people sheold have a voice in the points at issue. When the members of that House new that their monopolies end vented interests were liable to be tampered eith, they would place measure. befo e the country not with any ides of trusting the people hut to protect their own interests. They woold try Ay metre of their wealth to delude the people and afterwerds to misrepresent their verdict. Mr. Rigby maintained that in countries where the Referendum bad been applied the resolt bed beer a wholesale destruction of Government measure, both Radical and Conservative and he attributed that to the innate conservation—the hatred of change and innovation—of the penile. The Cons aaaa Use profewiel a great desire to give effect to the popular will, but they refused to lisle Ireland, .hor• the electors had for generations declared in favour of lion, Rule.</p>
