        <p>nre..l;t-11.1:11TON : It shoves how Libstal legi+lstion go.. v thsnugh.. .</p>
        <p>Mr. Mrs:kr:Jokes continuing. said the Cabinet met the night before the Bill semi laid 011 t he table. They bad ne time to Iran,, a fresh Bill—tlsughtert—but he had the ',lastest diffisulty in gPttiog his Cabinet to appear in the House to support the roamers of the,, Colonial Secretary. Mr. tV PtleNEss (Ex. Colonial Secretary, : The right bon. gentleman's memory is at fault. If I remember rightly—and I think his supporters will nor-</p>
        <p>robonste me—we spent three howl in !ramie, that BRA. Mr. Wyoov.Joon., I admit w• spent three boom tln discussing it and tailed to come to an agreement, hut we eubstituted one clause—and I main- tain that the whole of the discuseinit on this aide of the House revolved round that one clause. We would have mining to do with the rest of the Bill. Continuing. the Leader of the Opposition said that speaking for himself, he would never have anything to d., with reform of the House of Lords laxness he maintained that it was pest reform. A thing that was rotten to the core coati not be reformed. He was opposed to the Referendum root and branch. It was about the most undemocratic thing in the world. The Colonial Secretary professed to he a democrat, and yet he swam going to piece in the hands of the aristocrats an instrument that would enable them to throttle democracy in this country. Democracy, however, was not going to be throttled. The Coneerveticria wwship of democracy was lip. worship and nothing eke. They wou'd have to he pre- pared not only to woriMip democracy hot to obey it. The Prime Minieter had said that the Con PPPPP tives wished to get the 11011.9 of Lords question out of the way. Whet they really desired was to get democracy out of the way. It was admitted by careful students that the Referendum wars coneervatlve force. In this country it enact Si cqns.•xtive lona thet was wanted, but eonsethine that would accellorate reform. Ur. F. 0. TA 11,,,1t (Chancellor of tbe Exchequer) referred to the " disclosure, " of the Isaier of the Opposition with reference to Liberal Cabinet procedure. If Shot was a specimen of the may in which Liberal legislation was cooducted, it was no wonder that the country was not in sympathy with it. Ile area surprised to hear such condemnation of the Liberal measure for the reform of the House of Lords from the Leader of the Opposition, for when he Ira. Prime NI inisrer be wade, VehPITIPOL speech In support of it. (Laughter.) The Conservatives had no intention of going back on any pledge they hod Riven. They accepted the Amend- ment to th extent that they agreed that the }loose of Comm one should he atiorsme over Money Rills, but they laid that the Speaker shoold loon power to deter- , lee whet WM a Money Bill. With regard to the Referendum, the people ought to have the right to say whether they would have a measure or not. The speech of the Leader of the Opposition woe democrecy from beginning to rod, but it roan bogus democracy. Ile objected to matters being referred to fluv jaßr:itsle extlept at gsneral elections.</p>
        <p>dovr., My objection is not to the matter being referred to the people, but to the power to refer it to the pople being left in the hoods of single chamber. If :roe wive the people the power to denoted a. Ileforendion awl the House of Corereo es power to demand it, it would be a different thing entirely. The CIIA,ELLOR of the Ettntlettt'Re. : It only shows that the Lender of the Oppreiltion hes not taken the trouble to read the lest two clauses, heneuee there is a distinct provision—eel it is emphatic —that where the two louses diwigree. the measure shell lie role reed to ' the people unless the Government decides to adopt the alternative of renignstien. _</p>
        <p>: I ma•ntain that the right hon. gentles.o if misinterpreting what I bare said. The power to force a referonditin is in the heeds of the Lords of Parliament end of no one el.. The CHAN,F.I.I.OI‘ OF THE Eni'llitilUEß : I abaolittely nod entirely deny it.Contintiing the speaker maid thnt if they once got the Hon. of Lords out of the way there would be nothing between the people rind what the Government In power chose to force upon the country. There Wle no hnnert democracy in the proposal Co abolish the llouse of Lords end enhatitute nothing else. It was an imponible mtuatioa that e IL,uee of Loydn elected on the Budget, Free Trade or some other queetion, Omit] be •kle to introduce .y measure it liked, and ny approving it three time, encore that it should atitomationlly become law without any con- sultation of the electorate.</p>
