      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-04_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. --- " Miss Lancashire Limited."</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. --- " Miss Lancashire Limited."</p>
        <p>"Mica Llncaslii re Limited " has been presented in Warrington so frootuently that moot local playgoers have revelled its the rollicking and boisterous humour of this great farce. It ham, however, loot 11011 C of its , power to attract and amuse and there have been large audienees at the Royal Court Theatre this week to follow the adventures of Mary Ellen Thompson. the , Lancashire lace, in London. The fact that Iliac Florence Ila Melt, the pronrietreas of the company and the creator of the leading character, bis been pre- vented by temporary Ines of voice from appearing as Mary Ellen. has proved • great oisappointruent. Misr§ Frances Verena 14 an efficient understudy, although she has not mastered the proculiarites of the Lanca- shire dialect. and often lapses into conventional English. 'lie fun, however. sense, fast arid furions, Miss Verena throwing herself most enthusiastically into the spirit of the part, and it is one roar of laughter from beginning to end of the play. Mr Walter Passmore gives a particularly clever and life-like study r•I the Jew. Moses Goldberg, and Mr.</p>
        <p>Joe Redfern scums a notable success as the fussy lawyer. All the other parts are in capable hands. !Mr. David Donaldson portrays John Higgins, the much-avortied head of the fatuity, in eminently satisfactory style. Mr. Eric Vivian is a capital Harry Higgins ; Miss Norma Russell as Bertha sings acceptably ; and Mica Jessie l'atinan is a vivacious Eva Lancashire. Mr. Payne Fletcher deserves special praise for hits acting as the furiou4,loA-voiced I John Henry Thompson, and Miss Fanny Ohre is equally arousing as his Irish spouse. Sir. Henry !Lloyd also mite in some excellent work in the also! the butler. NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1911-02-04_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>'THE FACE AT THE WINDOW,</head>
        <p>'THE FACE AT THE WINDOW,</p>
        <p>! Next week at the Royal Court Theatre we are promised • visit from this great sensational French detective play. This piece hats had the honour of having been translated into several continental languages, and at present halfaslozen companies are touting the various countries cf the continent. The story of the career cf the notorious Le Loup is a regular carnival of crime</p>
        <p>his final undoing and retribution meted oat to him at the hatids of his great rival. the great detective. Paul Gooffet, is one which enthrals and thrills We can promise theatre-goer. threw hours en, itement, and also many good laughs, as the comedy element, which is based on the adventures of • cockney tourist in Paris, is cue which idlards many opportunities , for fun. ' VISIT OF MR. F. R. HENSON'S COMPANY.</p>
        <p>Local playgoers will he pleased to learn that the F. R. Benson Shalieoperean Company ~North) will nay iIS annual visit to Warrii.gton from February 13th to ISC.I. The con.pany. which include, thou. talented and popular sbakespereen performer. Mr. Hy. Herbert and Mt... Gladys Vendemee. will present the following attractive repertoire : Mo•day. "Hamlet "; Tueedav, " A Midsummer Night'. Dream' : IVednee- day.'• Twelfth Night " ; Thursday, "Tie Tempest" Friday, "She Stoops to Conquer "; Saturday. Richard the Third." Special matinee at 330 pm. On Saturday' A Midsummer Night's Dream."</p>
