        <head>CHILDRENS EFFORTS HELP THE GREENLEES FUND</head>
        <p>CHILDRENS EFFORTS HELP THE GREENLEES FUND</p>
        <p>A large and appreciative audience gathered at the Hall Cate, Stockton Meath, im Saturday lino, at the • invitation of Miss Gertrode Browne, to witness a progremme of dancing by her pupils. lho chair was occupied by 51r. E. 6. White, who opened The proceed- ings with an appropriate speech nod pointed 4Olt that none of the children had received nthre than two terms tuition and indeed sonic of them ranch less. The programme was of a very varied nnt.re,'lno nanhi round and square dances tying given in a very clever land delightful mariner try boys and girl. ranging in age from 7 to 12 years. Interspersed were • number of fancy dance., performed in •ppropriate oust woes. The I Flower Dance, in which half.boops, trimmed with gar- ' land. of various col&gt;urs of ruse., were swung toned fro to the rhythm of a delightfolly .Ireamy waltz, by four sweet little girls, proved a pretty picture. A little later, two tiny mains, aged 7, tripped blithely in, attired in natitical garb, end gave the Sailors Hof, pipe in such a StOtl.lOolike rummer a. to bring done the house and they had to repeat their performance,</p>
        <p>The seconl portion opt the programme commenced with the " fsrantella." detightfully danced by two girls and two boys clad in the national custom., of the country, front which thin dance eminsten. [be Gavotte, I too, proved very a:trimly., rho graceful movements and courtly drew.. of the performern being admirable. An encore was demanded for the pierette and pierrot " Flirtation' dance, execute,' inn manner not excelled hy pet:former. of riper Jeers. Goring the evenlnK . .</p>
        <p>sere:rat exquisite v.iolin 60104 tvere.7riven by Marina, um annealed by Miss Boonlair, and Mixv Purkinatin rendered two idensing pi•noforte solos. Miss Dolly Jon. pltyed very succimfollt for al/ the dances. ' The foliating took part in the fancy dancev i- -IXlistes Molly Coolison, Marjory Deakin, Ethel and Edith Melbourne nal May Wliite ; Maxtor. Willie Cookson and Harold Jaclosio. Urent credit is doe to ; Miro Browne for hitr efforts in teaching the children in !nook a thorough manner, and thins. marked by tlio Prnavntaition of a lovely bouquet from the Mind. of • ' tiny damsel, aged three, Slits Vera White, the daughter of the Chairman.</p>
        <p>The usual votes of thanks ware heartily accorded, at the close, on the rnotii.n of Mr. Booms Jackson seconded by Mr. Joseph Monk, rind collection in rid I of the Mayor of Warrington's Fund for the children of The late M-. Edward Greeolees realised Ll 6s. Gel.</p>
